WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.753
- Welcome back to this third panel,

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:17.103
Incarceration,
Decarceration, and Education,

00:00:18.396 --> 00:00:19.860
which is sort of formed around the notion

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.680
that universities in
particular have become centers

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.100
for prison education and reentry programs,

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.340
as well as abolitionist activity.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.490
How can we foster such
programs in the future,

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:36.720
and how might they relate to
the decarceration movement?

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.420
And our four panelists here,

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:42.390
I'm gonna introduce them
very briefly all at once

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.730
and then let them take over.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:47.250
First of all, Bernard Harcourt,

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:50.070
who's a multifaceted
intellectual and activist,

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:51.990
professor at Columbia Law,

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:55.020
founding director of the
Initiative for Justice Society

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:58.410
at Columbia Center for
Contemporary Critical Thought,

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:00.317
also, directeur d’études

00:01:00.317 --> 00:01:03.720
at the Ecole des Hautes
Etudes en Sciences Sociales

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.420
in Paris, the author of a dozen books,

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:10.830
brilliant editor of several
works by Michel Foucault,

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:13.770
lawyer for death row prisoners in Alabama,

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.963
and teacher at Rikers Island.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.530
And he'll be followed by Antonne Henshaw,

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:22.830
who is director of the
Transformative Justice Initiative,

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.800
which he founded to support
the formerly incarcerated

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.870
on their release from prison.

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:29.070
You can see him on videos

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:31.710
at the gate of East Jersey State Prison,

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:34.383
the first degree to
embrace the newly released.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.400
Antonne is also studying
for his master's degree

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.603
at the School of Criminal
Justice of Rutgers University.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:47.700
Next, Zelda Roland, Yale BA and PhD,

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.110
who is the founding director

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.810
of the Yale Prison Education Initiative,

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.490
where she almost single-handedly

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.110
has made an enormous success,

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.930
including the award of
a large enabling grant

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:00.690
from the Andrew Mellon Foundation.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.490
She coordinates the Yale program

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.980
with the Connecticut
Department of Corrections

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.380
and with a committed group
of Yale faculty and students

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:10.410
and with the University of New Haven.

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:13.350
And finally, our respondent, Ben Berger,

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:16.050
teaches political science at Swarthmore,

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.760
where he is executive director

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.630
of the Lang Center for Civic
and Social Responsibility.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:26.630
And in this role, he's
focused the center's mission

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:30.570
on connecting the curriculum
with the community.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:33.450
In particular, he's made prison education

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.360
a vital locus of engaged scholarship,

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.910
stressing the ways in
which students inside

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:42.000
and outside can co-create knowledge

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.750
and change public perceptions
and public policy.

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:46.800
So I'll say no more,

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.850
and turn things over to Bernard Harcourt.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:52.980
- Thank you, Peter,

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:57.980
and thank you all for an
amazing conversation so far.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:04.200
I'd like to start with a series

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:09.200
of epigraphs giving voice
to writings from the prison.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:17.237
First is from George Jackson,
"Soledad Brothers," 1970.

00:03:20.347 --> 00:03:23.400
"I met Marx, Lenin,
Trotsky, Engels, and Mao

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:27.480
when I entered prison,
and they redeemed me.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:28.560
For the first four years,

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.607
I studied nothing but
economics and military ideas."

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:39.723
From his brilliant book,
"Blood in My Eyes," 1971.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:42.963
George Jackson again.

00:03:44.437 --> 00:03:47.640
"I would be sitting in a
special locked isolation cell,

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.640
sometimes even with the lock welded shut,

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.160
and there would be no one to talk to,

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.260
just the sound of screaming voices.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:59.103
Because there's no human
contact, you depend on books.

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:04.920
No contact with people, special
lock welded on the door,

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.230
nobody around, I'm strictly by myself.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:10.023
The only friend I had was a book.

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:14.250
Sometimes I'd find myself
talking out loud to the author.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.110
I'd sort of wake myself

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:19.230
up and I'd hear myself
talking to this other person.

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:21.990
I guess it was some kind
of wish fulfillment.

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:22.950
When I'm asleep at night,

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:25.977
I still find myself
talking to those guys."

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:33.930
The next slide, yes, the next slide is,

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:37.563
the next epigraph is from Albert Woodfox,

00:04:39.304 --> 00:04:41.977
a brilliant author of
"Solitary", the book,

00:04:41.977 --> 00:04:44.460
"Solitary: Unbroken for Four Decades

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:45.630
in Solitary Confinement".

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:48.753
He spent four decades
in solitary confinement.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:52.653
Sadly, he passed away a
few months ago of COVID.

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:59.400
The years in prison had
worn his health down.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.617
He writes, in "Solitary",

00:05:03.577 --> 00:05:07.230
"I turned cell into a university,

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:10.080
a hall of debate, a law school.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.510
Reading was my salvation.

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:14.610
I first gravitated to books and authors

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:16.410
that dealt with politics and race.

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:20.310
George Jackson, Frantz Fanon,
Malcolm X, Marcus Garvey,

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:23.010
Steve Biko, Eldridge
Cleaver, 'Soul on Ice,'

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:25.740
J. A. Rogers' 'From Superman to Man.'

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:28.560
We read anything we could
find on slavery, communism,

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.020
socialism, Marxism, anti-imperialism,

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:32.610
the African independence movements,

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:35.130
and independence movements
from around the world.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:37.230
Leaning against my wall in the cell,

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:41.397
sitting on the floor, on my
bed, or at my table, I read."

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:49.790
The next epigraph is from a letter.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:54.930
It was a letter that was written

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:58.710
to Albert Woodfox by a man on death row.

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:00.780
And he'd sent it through his attorney,

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:02.850
George Kendall, an old friend,

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:05.400
and he'd sent a message to Mr. Woodfox

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.963
in this handwritten letter
dated December 9th, 2021.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:15.480
I tried to edit out
some passages that were,

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:19.740
but here's where he
writes to Albert Woodfox,

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:24.740
O.G Shaka Cinque, which
was his name inside.

00:06:25.957 --> 00:06:30.330
"I myself and some other
sociopolitically conscious comrades

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:32.640
have been having teach-in sessions

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:36.150
with your book, 'Solitary',
here on death row.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:38.310
It is deeply inspiring and motivational

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:40.380
and we wanted you to know this.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:42.300
An idea that just came to mind,

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.490
and you'll be able to see
a video of mine conducting

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:46.500
a demonstration on the 35th anniversary

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:48.330
of the assassination of George Jackson.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:50.910
Just know that there
are brothers down here

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.820
and who stand in that righteous tradition,

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:56.670
and we are still fighting on.

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:58.347
Guerilla love and respect."

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:07.130
Cycles of regenerative
action, regenerative cycles,

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:15.470
spontaneous chain reactions,
permanent revolutions.

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:20.913
What's so remarkable about prison writing,

00:07:22.528 --> 00:07:24.660
about prison reading, about prison study,

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:27.000
is the way in which the written word

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:29.923
in prison circulates and recirculates

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:33.990
and sparks and inspires and nourishes

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:37.503
and sustains new revolutionary cycles.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.110
George Jackson encountered Marx

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:44.227
and Mao in prison and became,
as Dr. Joy James says,

00:07:44.227 --> 00:07:47.640
"A dragon philosopher and
revolutionary abolitionist,

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:50.400
transforming himself
and his prison comrades

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.650
into the military wing
of a vanguard party,

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:53.970
The Black Panthers.

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:56.490
Albert Woodfox reads George Jackson,

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:58.890
right, as well as others,
"Mao's Little Red Book",

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:02.160
and he too is turned revolutionary,

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:04.470
co-founding the first official chapter,

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:06.660
The Black Panther Party behind bars.

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:08.640
Today, a person confined in death row

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:12.120
reads Albert Woodfox,
reading George Jackson,

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:15.720
and is rejuvenated in his
struggle against the oppression

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:18.780
of his confinement and
his sentence of death,

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:22.830
teach-in sessions in prison,
reading groups in prisons.

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:25.050
The cycle revolves rejuvenating

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:28.230
and transmitting the
passion, the commitment,

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:30.900
the struggle, the revolution,

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:33.773
generating new political praxis.

00:08:33.773 --> 00:08:38.773
What happened in California
in 2010 was simply remarkable

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:42.120
with the Short Corridor Collective,

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:45.450
which was formed in the SHU,

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:48.570
in the secured housing unit, in isolation,

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:51.393
basically, in Pelican State Prison.

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:56.760
That was a collection
of men held in solitary

00:08:56.760 --> 00:09:01.760
for being allegedly leaders
of different racial gangs,

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:05.520
including Todd Ashker, Arturo Castellanos,

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:10.110
Antonio Guillen, Satawa
Jama, Paul Redd and others.

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:12.060
And they formed a reading group

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:16.020
to discuss the works of
Bobby Sands and Cesar Chavez,

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:19.380
the Black Panthers, Michel
Foucault, and others.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:23.820
And as Professor Allegra McLeod recounts,

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:26.520
she writes, "Although the men
could not meet face to face

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:29.850
or even see one another
from their solitary cells,

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:32.520
they were able to organize
and read the same text,

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:35.070
pass notes, talk to one another,

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:39.420
pushing the pages torn from these books

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:41.640
through the vents and the drains

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:44.370
and the cracks in the walls

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:46.593
so that they could share the texts.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:49.710
And they were able to talk with each other

00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:54.710
through the vents and
discuss and formulate

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:57.150
an understanding of their situation,

00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:59.130
the way in which the prison administration

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:02.580
was using their racial
identities to control them.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:05.020
A long journey that ultimately led them

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:11.480
to organize the country's
largest ever prison hunger strike

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:15.870
in 2013, involving more than 30,000 women

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:20.870
and men throughout California
prisons who refused to eat.

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:28.527
And ultimately, leading to
litigation and other organizing

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:32.820
that ended indeterminate
solitary confinement

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:34.863
based on gang affiliations.

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:41.690
Reading Bobby Sands,
inspiring them to action.

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:44.820
Also, what happened,

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:49.260
we were talking earlier today
about Jean Genet, right?

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:51.867
Who helps form the group.

00:10:51.867 --> 00:10:53.070
(Bernard speaking in foreign language).

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:58.070
The Group of Prison Information in 1970,

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:02.700
reflecting particularly on George Jackson

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:04.590
on all facets of the prison

00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:07.200
and the repression at the time in France,

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:08.850
but also in the United States.

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:12.667
And ultimately, fueling this text called,

00:11:12.667 --> 00:11:15.630
"The assassination of George Jackson",

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:18.060
of which Genet wrote the preface,

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:21.130
but a text that would
be published in France

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:26.463
less than three months after
George Jackson's homicide,

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:31.200
right, as George Jackson
is killed on August,

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:35.730
I think it's 21, 1971,
and this book is published

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:40.673
by Galima with Jean Genet's
preface in November 10th of '71.

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:45.660
And it's remarkable, the way in which

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:49.140
all of these works inspired
others and philosophers

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:53.790
to rethink and rethink the
prison and rethink society

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:58.790
and had such extraordinary,
cyclical effects

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:02.580
on philosophers like Michel Foucault,

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:06.750
who, as a result of this
work and his engagement

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:10.533
in prison abolition in France at the time,

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:13.650
comes up with a completely different model

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:18.650
of our society based on civil war, right?

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:21.240
And he would write in
these important lectures

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:22.477
he delivered at the College de France.

00:12:22.477 --> 00:12:25.500
"The matrix of all struggles of power,

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:28.680
of all strategies of power,
and consequently also,

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:32.310
the matrix of all the struggles regarding

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:36.807
and against power is the
matrix of civil war."

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:43.410
So, one of the questions
we pose ourself, of course,

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:48.243
is how we engage in
education in the prison.

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:55.260
And what's clear to me, of
course, is as critical academics

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:57.720
in our relationship to prison education,

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:02.013
which is one of the most
important aspects of our lives,

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:05.490
what's remarkable is that
these prison writings

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:06.990
and experiences really convince

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:11.280
me that the most genuine
and authentic academy

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:16.280
is the one that's created by
the women and men behind bars,

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:19.560
in solitary, on the short corridor,

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:23.280
at Angola or Parchman or Rikers, right,

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:26.490
in their reading groups and teach-ins

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:28.383
and their critical examination.

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:34.350
People behind bars form the true academy.

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:35.460
And you can hear this.

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:38.940
You can hear this in
solitary, you can see it.

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:41.253
It's tangible. You can feel it.

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:47.647
When a friend, man, friend
and man, Darryl Robertson,

00:13:49.950 --> 00:13:52.593
is released from Rikers Island,

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:56.610
walking out of the New York
County Criminal Courthouse

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:59.460
at 100 Center Street
in downtown Manhattan,

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:04.460
carrying, as his only
property, his only possessions,

00:14:05.192 --> 00:14:10.192
275-pound bags of books,

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:15.810
more than 100 books on
his back is the only thing

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:19.143
that had got him through
his time at Rikers.

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:23.160
It becomes clear that these
spaces built to confine,

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:26.580
to punish, to do violence
to men and women,

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:29.970
these spaces have turned
into, are turned into,

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:31.680
by the people who are prisoned there,

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:33.450
the true and genuine academy,

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:37.920
where critical thought and
debate is really most alive.

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:42.270
Sometimes I feel like our ivory
towers pale in comparison.

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:45.030
There isn't the same drive almost.

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:46.770
There isn't the same hunger,

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:48.600
there isn't the same need to read,

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:51.603
to critique, to engage in praxis.

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:57.720
And so moving forward, and as we think

00:14:57.720 --> 00:15:02.310
about kind of education
and justice and education,

00:15:02.310 --> 00:15:04.050
I think it's not always the question

00:15:04.050 --> 00:15:06.180
of what we academics contribute

00:15:06.180 --> 00:15:08.820
to the critique and praxis
of men and women behind bars,

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:11.880
but instead, how can
we, in higher education,

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:14.910
transform ourselves to better model

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:17.850
the relationship between
critique and praxis

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:22.443
in the true academy that
the people in prison create.

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:25.683
One final thought.

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:31.803
These prison writings, they
emerge out of the depths,

00:15:33.487 --> 00:15:38.487
"De Profundis", as Oscar Wilde called it.

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:44.043
George Jackson himself
wrote from those depths,

00:15:45.367 --> 00:15:48.630
"Capture," he said,
"imprisonment is the closest

00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:52.803
to being dead that one is likely
to experience in this life.

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:57.723
Oscar Wilde also wrote from those depths.

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:02.163
And during his time at the Reading Prison,

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:04.680
he was so deeply affected

00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:06.720
by the execution of a fellow prisoner,

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:11.720
he wrote "The Ballad of Reading
Gaol", the Reading jail.

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:14.460
On his release after two years

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:17.250
of incarceration for the
crime of homosexuality,

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:22.140
he signed it C33, that
was his prison number.

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:25.323
And so it appeared,
originally, anonymously.

00:16:26.190 --> 00:16:31.170
I think I'll finish with a
verse from that beautiful text.

00:16:33.607 --> 00:16:35.880
"This too, I know.

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:38.883
And why, as it were, if
each could know the same,

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:42.450
that every prison that men build

00:16:42.450 --> 00:16:45.720
is built with bricks of shame

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:50.430
and bound with bars lest Christ should see

00:16:50.430 --> 00:16:53.673
how men, their brothers, maim.

00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:55.823
Thank you.

00:16:57.752 --> 00:17:00.919
(audience applauding)

00:17:04.616 --> 00:17:06.773
- Do you wanna speak here or
at the podium? Either way.

00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:14.730
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:19.890
I'm in a peculiar position

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:22.713
'cause I'm blessed to be here though.

00:17:24.330 --> 00:17:27.753
I am a lifer.

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:30.480
I served 100% of a 30 year

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:33.330
to life sentence in New Jersey's prisons.

00:17:33.330 --> 00:17:38.330
I was incarcerated in 1988,
and I was released in 2018.

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:45.190
As I listened all day, it's amazing how

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:49.590
what we call inside, liberation education,

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:51.720
makes this day possible.

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:55.470
So for one, I'm humbled, I am blessed,

00:17:55.470 --> 00:17:57.840
and I thank you all for creating a space

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:02.073
where I'm safe and I'm able to heal,

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:05.883
and that we can heal together.

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:09.330
Liberation education.

00:18:09.330 --> 00:18:11.520
I grew up in an environment,

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:16.260
if you can imagine, where I
wasn't supposed to survive.

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:20.310
I was five, four, 118 pounds,
never incarcerated before,

00:18:20.310 --> 00:18:23.820
and I was sentenced to 30 years to life.

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:25.680
And I didn't go to a youth facility.

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:28.680
I went straight to an adult male facility,

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:31.030
and I was sent there
to die or to be fodder

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:34.413
for the other bigger, stronger people.

00:18:36.750 --> 00:18:41.750
But I remember meeting two
men at the orientation,

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:45.153
and they asked me a question.

00:18:46.590 --> 00:18:49.140
What are you gonna do, play
basketball and be in a yard

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:51.630
or go to school and get an education?

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:53.310
Prior to my incarceration,

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:55.953
education has always been my safe space.

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:02.880
My mother abandoned me and
my brother and sisters twice.

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:05.553
My father raised us, he
was an authoritarian.

00:19:07.530 --> 00:19:10.740
And it led to a lot of what I used

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:14.013
to make decisions that
led me to incarceration.

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:18.180
But when those two men asked
me what was I gonna do,

00:19:18.180 --> 00:19:22.770
I immediately remembered my
elementary school principal,

00:19:22.770 --> 00:19:25.017
Ms. Fenderson, and I said, "School!"

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:30.860
I went to school and I got my GED

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:34.470
within six months after entering prison.

00:19:34.470 --> 00:19:37.170
And then I enrolled in a college program

00:19:37.170 --> 00:19:38.660
at Mercer County Community College

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:43.590
in New Jersey to get
an associate's degree.

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:48.590
I remember the first
semester, I got two A's.

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:53.340
I really didn't think I was
college material, but I was.

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:57.570
And I went to sign up
for the next semester,

00:19:57.570 --> 00:19:59.070
and the Pell Grants were gone.

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:02.280
And immediately I'm like,
"What are you doing?"

00:20:02.280 --> 00:20:06.570
I finally find something
I like, I enjoy, I'm safe,

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:08.760
and I'm in a maximum security prison.

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:10.800
But I quickly learned

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:13.750
that the most violent people
don't like to be around books.

00:20:15.450 --> 00:20:17.190
Right, if you're in the library,

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:21.450
if you are in the law library,

00:20:21.450 --> 00:20:24.300
they don't go around books
or classrooms too tough.

00:20:24.300 --> 00:20:26.409
They hang around on the cell blocks

00:20:26.409 --> 00:20:29.460
and they wanna run the block,
they wanna run the phone,

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:30.840
they wanna run the mess hall,

00:20:30.840 --> 00:20:32.370
they wanna control the haircuts,

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:35.670
and all the things that are,
don't really mean anything.

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:36.930
All I cared about was,

00:20:36.930 --> 00:20:40.323
is that clock gonna keep
moving so I can get outta here?

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:42.600
I was blessed to be

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:46.500
around my prison father,
Mr. Jerome Perkins.

00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:48.660
And I began to quickly learn

00:20:48.660 --> 00:20:53.567
from him the pearls of
structural violence, right,

00:20:54.630 --> 00:20:57.630
and how systems became
structurally violent,

00:20:57.630 --> 00:21:00.360
whether you participated in 'em or not.

00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:02.070
So we began to chart a course

00:21:02.070 --> 00:21:03.423
to get education back in prison

00:21:03.423 --> 00:21:07.410
that took us 28 years to get it back.

00:21:07.410 --> 00:21:12.410
And I remember getting higher education

00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:17.430
back into New Jersey State prisons

00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:20.130
by writing a white
paper to the legislators

00:21:20.130 --> 00:21:25.130
that went in a slew of
bills 4202 in New Jersey.

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:28.410
And they took the section
to make education mandatory

00:21:28.410 --> 00:21:31.683
in all New Jersey's
prisons as it was written.

00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:38.040
And I remember, she's
an old, she's assembly,

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:39.480
she was assembly woman then,

00:21:39.480 --> 00:21:44.480
she is now in the House
of Representatives,

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:47.220
then assembly woman,
Bonnie Watson Coleman,

00:21:47.220 --> 00:21:51.450
and a few, Grace Spencer and
Cohen and a few others came in,

00:21:51.450 --> 00:21:53.970
and we did our presentation,

00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:58.560
and education became mandatory
inside New Jersey's prisons.

00:21:58.560 --> 00:22:00.090
Did I ever think that that was possible,

00:22:00.090 --> 00:22:01.680
that they would listen to us?

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:04.170
And then we began to see that it wasn't

00:22:04.170 --> 00:22:09.170
that we inside the carceral
space didn't have voices,

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:11.790
it was that our voices were silence.

00:22:11.790 --> 00:22:13.020
And it took people coming

00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:17.820
from the outside to the inside
to magnify those voices.

00:22:17.820 --> 00:22:20.760
And so the senators and the
legislators started coming in,

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:23.337
and they began to take
the lead from the people

00:22:23.337 --> 00:22:25.713
and our families that
were directly involved.

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:27.660
We get to the point

00:22:27.660 --> 00:22:31.443
where I meet this wonderful
man, Mr. Peter Brooks.

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:35.520
I'm working, at the time,
I was working on my BA,

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:37.140
but at the same time,

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:39.030
I was going through the parole process,

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:41.610
which was the most depressing

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:44.550
and hard thing that I ever had to do.

00:22:44.550 --> 00:22:48.210
And Mr. Brooks brought
in a detective class

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:51.720
on detective and
interrogations and techniques.

00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:53.760
And I remember reading

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:57.693
all of these different
tropes that he brought in.

00:22:58.530 --> 00:23:01.260
And I remember my first interview

00:23:01.260 --> 00:23:03.870
with the New Jersey State Parole Board.

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:08.850
And two days prior to that
interview, Professor Brooks,

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:10.440
he don't like me calling him that though,

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:13.800
but at the time, Professor Brooks brought

00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:18.800
in this video on the Reid
technique of interrogation.

00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:25.590
And when I walked into the
room of the two-man panel,

00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:28.770
they immediately began
using the Reid technique

00:23:28.770 --> 00:23:31.923
of interrogation in my parole hearing.

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:35.970
And two days prior, we have to write

00:23:35.970 --> 00:23:39.960
a reflection paper and an
analysis on this stuff.

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:42.477
I'm sitting there like,
"Are they serious?"

00:23:45.972 --> 00:23:49.487
But that's what we call
liberation education, right?

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:55.050
If you're not religious-oriented,
but I'm very spiritual,

00:23:55.050 --> 00:23:58.724
but I say there was some
divine intervention there,

00:23:58.724 --> 00:24:01.440
I don't know what to
call it or what it was,

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:04.260
but it came in a moment
when I needed the most

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:07.080
to be able to understand
what was about to happen

00:24:07.080 --> 00:24:09.750
and how the structural
violence of a system

00:24:09.750 --> 00:24:11.550
was gonna be perpetuated against

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:13.713
me if I wasn't prepared educationally.

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:20.520
So that system was able to push me through

00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:23.853
to the final level of the full board.

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:28.470
And the way the full board went

00:24:28.470 --> 00:24:31.173
was totally different
than the two-man panel.

00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:35.610
Now, and there's, we know
there's sharks in a room,

00:24:35.610 --> 00:24:37.020
so they're waiting to jump out.

00:24:37.020 --> 00:24:39.300
It's 12 people, and two of the 12

00:24:39.300 --> 00:24:42.240
are going to attack you
for absolutely no reason

00:24:42.240 --> 00:24:44.490
and try to provoke an emotional response

00:24:44.490 --> 00:24:46.950
to justify keeping you in prison.

00:24:46.950 --> 00:24:49.320
Now mind you, I said I'm a lifer.

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:51.240
That means I'm on parole
for the rest of my life.

00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:54.030
I'm still a slave of
the state of New Jersey.

00:24:54.030 --> 00:24:56.103
I have a travel pass to get here.

00:24:57.420 --> 00:25:02.420
And Professor Ben Berger
had to be my escort

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:06.270
in order for my plantation
owners in New Jersey

00:25:06.270 --> 00:25:09.183
to give me permission
to be in Connecticut.

00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:12.360
So for anybody that
thinks slavery is over,

00:25:12.360 --> 00:25:14.970
it is not, right?

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:19.970
This is why we have this national
campaign, Amendment 13th,

00:25:20.160 --> 00:25:22.080
to remove the exception clause

00:25:22.080 --> 00:25:23.790
from our national constitution

00:25:23.790 --> 00:25:26.883
and state constitution all
throughout the country.

00:25:27.780 --> 00:25:31.200
So as we began to go through this process

00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:33.510
in the New Jersey State Parole Board,

00:25:33.510 --> 00:25:36.080
they began to ask me, you know,

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:38.190
in the Reid technique, they
make you feel comfortable.

00:25:38.190 --> 00:25:42.660
Let's talk about your
early childhood life,

00:25:42.660 --> 00:25:44.730
your best memories, and things like that.

00:25:44.730 --> 00:25:46.530
Then all of a sudden, wham,

00:25:46.530 --> 00:25:48.480
let's talk about the day of your crime.

00:25:49.830 --> 00:25:52.980
No segue, no nothing,
we just gonna get in it.

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:56.730
What was you thinking?
What were you feeling?

00:25:56.730 --> 00:26:01.730
Now, let's remember, during
an entire 30-year process,

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:03.210
you are surviving.

00:26:03.210 --> 00:26:05.220
You are not thinking about the very thing

00:26:05.220 --> 00:26:06.873
that led you to that moment.

00:26:08.460 --> 00:26:12.420
And now in this interview,
because it's not a hearing,

00:26:12.420 --> 00:26:17.130
it's a interview to see
if you are qualified

00:26:17.130 --> 00:26:19.500
to serve the discretionary portion

00:26:19.500 --> 00:26:21.423
of your sentence in the community.

00:26:22.410 --> 00:26:24.363
It is a privilege, this is not a right.

00:26:26.340 --> 00:26:31.340
So we're going to see if our
carceral slave can come back

00:26:31.710 --> 00:26:36.710
out amongst us under a
convict leasing type system

00:26:37.530 --> 00:26:41.970
that we still have social
control over this person.

00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:43.650
Sounds like a lot of philosophy, don't it?

00:26:43.650 --> 00:26:47.703
Foucault, Hobbs, Locke, right?

00:26:49.770 --> 00:26:52.680
But in order to associate that,

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:56.550
we have to have the academies,

00:26:56.550 --> 00:26:59.430
with the academies that are inside,

00:26:59.430 --> 00:27:02.883
building liberation education models,

00:27:03.870 --> 00:27:06.870
to end the structural violence of systems.

00:27:06.870 --> 00:27:09.660
So I remember,

00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:12.780
I got to one of the
sharks and he was a psych.

00:27:12.780 --> 00:27:14.970
90% of the New Jersey State Parole Board

00:27:14.970 --> 00:27:17.553
are former law enforcement.

00:27:18.450 --> 00:27:21.933
So you have to know what
audience you're speaking to.

00:27:22.830 --> 00:27:25.837
And he said to me,

00:27:25.837 --> 00:27:29.670
"You're a liar. Everything
you said is a farce.

00:27:29.670 --> 00:27:33.210
It doesn't make sense.
You're in here deceiving us."

00:27:33.210 --> 00:27:37.320
And he just went on with
a litany of insults.

00:27:37.320 --> 00:27:39.600
And I sat there.

00:27:39.600 --> 00:27:43.057
And he asked a question later on, he said,

00:27:43.057 --> 00:27:46.020
"Well, you don't have anything to say?"

00:27:46.020 --> 00:27:46.957
And I simply said,

00:27:46.957 --> 00:27:49.607
"I'm here to answer questions.
You made a statement."

00:27:52.440 --> 00:27:55.980
He said, so like you
guys just did, he said,

00:27:55.980 --> 00:27:58.770
and the people in the room
all did the same thing.

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:01.107
His own colleagues was like, "He's right."

00:28:02.340 --> 00:28:07.340
But that becomes critically
aware of a liberation education

00:28:08.940 --> 00:28:12.060
to not get dragged into a conversation

00:28:12.060 --> 00:28:16.380
that's mainly built on
accusations and not questions.

00:28:16.380 --> 00:28:19.200
I'm here to answer
questions, nothing else.

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:20.463
So he said,

00:28:20.463 --> 00:28:22.787
"Well, what do you think
about what I said about you?"

00:28:24.731 --> 00:28:25.564
(audience chuckling)

00:28:25.564 --> 00:28:28.140
Now again, be careful what
you send across the net.

00:28:28.140 --> 00:28:30.003
You might not like what comes back.

00:28:31.862 --> 00:28:33.817
And so I answered him, I said,

00:28:33.817 --> 00:28:37.530
"I know that if I lie, if I'm deceptive,

00:28:37.530 --> 00:28:39.603
if I'm not forthcoming in any way,

00:28:40.620 --> 00:28:43.320
that keeps me in prison
for the rest of my life,

00:28:43.320 --> 00:28:45.483
and I would not waste your time nor mine.

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:48.780
And if you feel as though I'm lying

00:28:48.780 --> 00:28:52.287
and that I'm being deceptive,
I'm sorry you feel that way."

00:28:53.820 --> 00:28:55.860
Because I wasn't speaking to one person,

00:28:55.860 --> 00:28:59.070
I was speaking to 12, and
the majority who voted

00:28:59.070 --> 00:29:03.153
would either vote to keep me
in prison or to release me.

00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:08.850
But I remember the Sherlock Holmes

00:29:08.850 --> 00:29:13.850
and the detective novels that
Peter Brooks brought inside

00:29:15.090 --> 00:29:20.090
and how the questions were being asked

00:29:20.670 --> 00:29:24.600
and answered to get to a
particular result, right?

00:29:24.600 --> 00:29:26.070
So it's about that, you know,

00:29:26.070 --> 00:29:30.000
that dance to not say what
I think they wanna hear,

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:32.280
but to exercise my own agency

00:29:32.280 --> 00:29:35.190
and my own liberation
and take it in my hands,

00:29:35.190 --> 00:29:37.500
and whatever happens, I
can't control the outcome.

00:29:37.500 --> 00:29:40.863
It's not my decision, but
I can control my process.

00:29:42.660 --> 00:29:44.040
That's it.

00:29:44.040 --> 00:29:45.480
And it's the same way with education

00:29:45.480 --> 00:29:46.770
and school and things like that.

00:29:46.770 --> 00:29:48.540
I can just put forth the best ideas

00:29:48.540 --> 00:29:51.627
that I can in a piece of, on
a paper or things like that.

00:29:51.627 --> 00:29:53.010
But what I find,

00:29:53.010 --> 00:29:55.920
what was the hardest thing
for me was coming home,

00:29:55.920 --> 00:29:59.643
I was punished inside for writing.

00:30:01.470 --> 00:30:05.220
I did seven years on and
off during my 30 years

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:08.313
in isolated confinement because
of my political writings.

00:30:09.180 --> 00:30:10.560
I have yet to write anything

00:30:10.560 --> 00:30:13.083
of my own thought when I came home.

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:16.980
Because I'm on parole, and
if they came in my house,

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:18.630
they could pull it and they could use

00:30:18.630 --> 00:30:20.763
that as justification of putting me back.

00:30:22.620 --> 00:30:25.680
So I'm not free, though
I'm physically free.

00:30:25.680 --> 00:30:30.680
I was sharing with Ben, the last,

00:30:31.200 --> 00:30:32.430
I have a new parole officer.

00:30:32.430 --> 00:30:35.400
I have had six in four
years, and I'm on level four.

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:38.730
And what that means is I
see my parole officer once.

00:30:38.730 --> 00:30:41.850
I would see him four
times a year, quarterly.

00:30:41.850 --> 00:30:45.397
But I have a new parole
officer, and his thing is,

00:30:45.397 --> 00:30:46.863
"That's the minimum standard.

00:30:47.970 --> 00:30:50.820
I can see you as many times
as I want, and this and that."

00:30:50.820 --> 00:30:52.140
I said, "Well, I have a standard,

00:30:52.140 --> 00:30:53.520
if you can just do whatever you want,

00:30:53.520 --> 00:30:55.620
you have broad discretion."

00:30:55.620 --> 00:30:58.173
So we got into this back and forth thing.

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:02.583
So he came and did a home
visit to my apartment.

00:31:03.510 --> 00:31:06.930
And when he walked in,
he came in triggering.

00:31:06.930 --> 00:31:08.670
I'm very conscious about my triggers.

00:31:08.670 --> 00:31:09.503
And when I'm triggered,

00:31:09.503 --> 00:31:12.120
I will let people know
around me that I'm triggered,

00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:13.860
and I'm not being aggressive or anything.

00:31:13.860 --> 00:31:15.990
But, and I said that to him.

00:31:15.990 --> 00:31:19.323
And so we get up in my
apartment and this guy says,

00:31:21.277 --> 00:31:24.150
"Well, I'll be back next month."

00:31:24.150 --> 00:31:25.350
I said, "No, you won't."

00:31:26.820 --> 00:31:28.950
And he says, "Well, you are on parole."

00:31:28.950 --> 00:31:30.662
I said, "Yes I am."

00:31:30.662 --> 00:31:31.890
And I said, "I've earned level four

00:31:31.890 --> 00:31:35.490
to not have to see you every month."

00:31:35.490 --> 00:31:36.513
And then he said,

00:31:38.287 --> 00:31:40.487
"Well, I feel like
you're being aggressive."

00:31:41.580 --> 00:31:46.357
And he said, "I'm
concerned for my safety."

00:31:49.770 --> 00:31:54.003
And he stepped back and put
his hand on his service weapon.

00:31:55.350 --> 00:31:58.347
And when he did it, I
was like, "Oh, snap."

00:31:59.940 --> 00:32:04.710
So I walked into my kitchen,
and he walked towards me,

00:32:04.710 --> 00:32:06.637
and I said to him, I said,

00:32:06.637 --> 00:32:09.630
"I thought you said that you were afraid

00:32:09.630 --> 00:32:11.820
for your safety and that
I was being aggressive.

00:32:11.820 --> 00:32:14.280
I'm trying to give you space
so you don't feel that way."

00:32:14.280 --> 00:32:17.212
He says, "Imma need you to sit down."

00:32:17.212 --> 00:32:18.212
Now I'm in my house.

00:32:19.830 --> 00:32:22.590
So I said, "I'm not gonna do that."

00:32:22.590 --> 00:32:24.900
I said, "Are you done with your search?

00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:27.300
Are you done with whatever
you came here to do?"

00:32:28.290 --> 00:32:30.810
Now we're talking liberation education.

00:32:30.810 --> 00:32:33.363
Let's, like, really think about this.

00:32:35.160 --> 00:32:39.960
I am telling a person in authority now

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:41.700
to get outta my house.

00:32:41.700 --> 00:32:46.700
He takes his flashlight and
began to search my house

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:50.850
and my living room and says,
"Do you have me on YouTube?"

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:56.490
So I'm like, "You're really triggering."

00:32:56.490 --> 00:32:58.710
So I open the living
room, I open the door,

00:32:58.710 --> 00:33:01.237
'cause it's right by my
living room, and I say,

00:33:01.237 --> 00:33:02.457
"You have to leave."

00:33:03.660 --> 00:33:06.367
But then later on, I call
Professor Ben and was like,

00:33:06.367 --> 00:33:09.660
"Yo, I don't know what this
guy's gonna do. I don't know."

00:33:09.660 --> 00:33:13.320
But immediately, with our
education and how we do things,

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:18.320
one thing is, due process rights, right?

00:33:18.360 --> 00:33:20.280
I'm only asking for what I'm entitled

00:33:20.280 --> 00:33:23.160
to by due process and law.

00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:24.780
I'm not asking for anything extra.

00:33:24.780 --> 00:33:28.683
I'm not asking for any
particular special treatment.

00:33:31.230 --> 00:33:35.370
The beautiful thing about
everything that happened is,

00:33:35.370 --> 00:33:38.700
was the controversy and the abuse

00:33:38.700 --> 00:33:43.680
of discretion allowed
me to navigate safely

00:33:43.680 --> 00:33:45.840
using liberation education

00:33:45.840 --> 00:33:48.303
that allowed me to survive 30 years,

00:33:50.400 --> 00:33:53.670
and how to turn that
into a transferable skill

00:33:53.670 --> 00:33:58.670
on the street and coming out
and saying, I, if need be,

00:34:00.570 --> 00:34:03.690
how I survived 30 years through books,

00:34:03.690 --> 00:34:08.690
through those very things that
you talked about, book clubs.

00:34:09.090 --> 00:34:11.880
We had over 1,500 books in
East Jersey State Prison.

00:34:11.880 --> 00:34:13.863
We have our own library that we built.

00:34:15.090 --> 00:34:17.613
They banned the new Jim Crow. Really?

00:34:19.050 --> 00:34:22.350
Like "Blood In My Eye," if
you got caught with that,

00:34:22.350 --> 00:34:25.473
it is six months solitary confinement.

00:34:27.270 --> 00:34:29.720
Contraband immediately.
You're going to the hole.

00:34:30.960 --> 00:34:31.793
Right?

00:34:33.030 --> 00:34:38.030
But my grandmother,
who is Native American,

00:34:41.220 --> 00:34:46.140
she would introduce me to Red Cloud.

00:34:46.140 --> 00:34:47.580
And Red Cloud had a saying

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:51.397
that I used every day
while I served 30 years,

00:34:51.397 --> 00:34:53.397
"Every day is a good day to die."

00:34:56.820 --> 00:34:59.613
Every day is a good day to die.

00:35:02.310 --> 00:35:06.090
And I thank, I never really
got to thank Mr. Peter

00:35:06.090 --> 00:35:11.090
for providing that lesson in that season

00:35:13.950 --> 00:35:17.160
to liberate me from incarceration.

00:35:17.160 --> 00:35:17.993
Thank you.

00:35:19.100 --> 00:35:22.267
(audience applauding)

00:35:35.250 --> 00:35:36.450
- I'm gonna stay sitting

00:35:36.450 --> 00:35:40.320
just to acknowledge
that I'm on this level.

00:35:40.320 --> 00:35:43.263
You're at that level. That
was amazing. Thank you.

00:35:45.039 --> 00:35:48.150
I think that was really important.

00:35:48.150 --> 00:35:50.253
Can you hear me? Let's see.

00:35:56.160 --> 00:36:00.870
All right, I wanna, of course,
thank Peter for having me,

00:36:00.870 --> 00:36:04.473
inviting me, organizing
this incredible symposium.

00:36:06.480 --> 00:36:10.260
I wanna really process and appreciate

00:36:10.260 --> 00:36:14.253
both of the panelists here and also,

00:36:15.390 --> 00:36:17.553
in advance, thank you
for being kind to me.

00:36:20.850 --> 00:36:23.280
And I also, I really
quickly wanna acknowledge

00:36:23.280 --> 00:36:26.670
and thank all of the faculty here

00:36:26.670 --> 00:36:29.733
who've taught in our
prison education program,

00:36:30.810 --> 00:36:35.520
who serve on our faculty
advisory committees,

00:36:35.520 --> 00:36:38.673
Caleb Smith, Elizabeth
Hinton, Randall Horton,

00:36:40.200 --> 00:36:44.193
and also fellows and alumni of YPEI here,

00:36:45.600 --> 00:36:47.040
people who've worked with our program.

00:36:47.040 --> 00:36:48.420
A lot of familiar faces here,

00:36:48.420 --> 00:36:52.230
so I'm really glad that I'm gonna spare

00:36:52.230 --> 00:36:57.230
you my typical fundraising presentation.

00:36:59.097 --> 00:37:01.770
(audience laughing)

00:37:01.770 --> 00:37:04.200
Also, I see a few
members of Stop Solitary,

00:37:04.200 --> 00:37:05.763
which is cool, yeah.

00:37:07.380 --> 00:37:09.900
So I'm punching above my weight

00:37:09.900 --> 00:37:11.880
here a little bit on this panel.

00:37:11.880 --> 00:37:15.480
I'm what you would call a
practitioner, or if you're being

00:37:15.480 --> 00:37:18.393
a little less charitable,
an administrator.

00:37:20.010 --> 00:37:22.200
I imagine I was asked to sit on this panel

00:37:22.200 --> 00:37:24.330
because I have something to say,

00:37:24.330 --> 00:37:27.600
not just as someone who runs
a prison education program,

00:37:27.600 --> 00:37:30.630
but especially also as someone who figured

00:37:30.630 --> 00:37:35.340
out how to build one at the
most unlikely of places,

00:37:35.340 --> 00:37:37.923
the university where we gather today.

00:37:40.530 --> 00:37:45.273
And the short story is, we
found the glitch in the matrix.

00:37:47.190 --> 00:37:50.310
What I'd like to do with
my brief time is telling

00:37:50.310 --> 00:37:53.880
you just a little bit about
the program here at Yale

00:37:53.880 --> 00:37:56.280
and through our partnership
with University of New Haven

00:37:56.280 --> 00:38:00.723
and what it is that we do for context,

00:38:01.980 --> 00:38:03.960
and then highlight some
details and vignettes

00:38:03.960 --> 00:38:06.300
from the overlap of the twin institutions

00:38:06.300 --> 00:38:07.770
of the prison and the university

00:38:07.770 --> 00:38:11.130
that touch on the larger
theme of the symposium,

00:38:11.130 --> 00:38:14.670
incarceration and imagination,
and also this panel,

00:38:14.670 --> 00:38:15.510
which has been titled,

00:38:15.510 --> 00:38:18.483
Incarceration,
Decarceration, and Education.

00:38:19.620 --> 00:38:23.010
And because I work deeply within
institutional bureaucracies

00:38:23.010 --> 00:38:24.420
of the prison and the university,

00:38:24.420 --> 00:38:27.240
one of the things I set
out to do for myself

00:38:27.240 --> 00:38:28.800
and agreeing to sit on this panel

00:38:28.800 --> 00:38:32.250
is use it as an opportunity
for my own reflection

00:38:32.250 --> 00:38:35.220
about the acts of imagination that were

00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:39.333
and are required in order
for this program to exist.

00:38:41.190 --> 00:38:45.000
So the program was, in
a way, in its own way,

00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:47.313
created by incarcerated students.

00:38:48.960 --> 00:38:51.870
The seed of origin

00:38:51.870 --> 00:38:54.870
is through Wesleyan Center
for Prison Education,

00:38:54.870 --> 00:38:59.700
where myself and other
faculty and graduate students

00:38:59.700 --> 00:39:04.500
at Yale had been volunteering
for years and years.

00:39:04.500 --> 00:39:08.040
Around the time that I started
volunteering at Cheshire

00:39:08.040 --> 00:39:12.060
through Wesleyan's prison
education program in 2014,

00:39:12.060 --> 00:39:13.230
it was a semester,

00:39:13.230 --> 00:39:15.660
I was supposed to be
writing my dissertation.

00:39:15.660 --> 00:39:20.313
So, of course, I was looking
for any excuse for distraction.

00:39:22.230 --> 00:39:26.610
I kept finding on campus that there were

00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:28.260
a lot of different faculty here

00:39:28.260 --> 00:39:31.323
who were really invested
in this, who were excited,

00:39:32.220 --> 00:39:34.950
who were excited about teaching in prison,

00:39:34.950 --> 00:39:36.750
who thought it was really important,

00:39:37.770 --> 00:39:42.770
and that it really made
serious their vocation

00:39:45.030 --> 00:39:49.173
in a way that teaching
on campus maybe hadn't.

00:39:50.700 --> 00:39:53.340
And so as I started going in more and more

00:39:53.340 --> 00:39:54.717
and meeting more of these faculty

00:39:54.717 --> 00:39:57.180
and graduate students
who were deeply invested

00:39:57.180 --> 00:39:59.340
in Wesleyan's program or Bard's program

00:39:59.340 --> 00:40:00.903
at the Bard Prison Initiative,

00:40:02.340 --> 00:40:04.500
the students at Wesleyan encouraged

00:40:04.500 --> 00:40:07.203
me to start a program at Yale.

00:40:08.490 --> 00:40:11.700
They knew that, well, first they thought

00:40:11.700 --> 00:40:14.010
there's a lot of prisons in this state,

00:40:14.010 --> 00:40:16.560
and a lot of them don't
have programs like ours.

00:40:16.560 --> 00:40:18.570
And if you could start a program at Yale,

00:40:18.570 --> 00:40:21.090
it would really benefit a lot of people.

00:40:21.090 --> 00:40:22.020
This was at Cheshire.

00:40:22.020 --> 00:40:24.450
And they said some of us had
to transfer from other prisons

00:40:24.450 --> 00:40:27.363
just for the shot at
applying to this program.

00:40:28.290 --> 00:40:33.290
But they also, in that
program, knew that a program

00:40:33.330 --> 00:40:36.810
at Yale would have more
than just a direct impact.

00:40:36.810 --> 00:40:39.420
It would have a national impact.

00:40:39.420 --> 00:40:43.860
It would inspire other
institutions of higher learning,

00:40:43.860 --> 00:40:46.260
competitive institutions
who, as we all know,

00:40:46.260 --> 00:40:50.310
only do things to outdo each other,

00:40:50.310 --> 00:40:52.290
that it would create this ripple effect.

00:40:52.290 --> 00:40:54.390
And that it could be hugely important.

00:40:54.390 --> 00:40:56.040
If we could start a program at Yale,

00:40:56.040 --> 00:40:59.017
other programs could look to us and say,

00:40:59.017 --> 00:41:00.957
"Yale did it, we can, too."

00:41:02.520 --> 00:41:04.230
And that was inspiring to me

00:41:04.230 --> 00:41:08.103
to think about the possibilities
if we could get that far.

00:41:09.240 --> 00:41:12.780
So a few of us started gathering together,

00:41:12.780 --> 00:41:15.630
the faculty, I wanna call out Ron Jenkins

00:41:15.630 --> 00:41:19.050
who's been teaching in
prison for a long time.

00:41:19.050 --> 00:41:22.470
Ron was there at our first meeting

00:41:22.470 --> 00:41:25.590
of Yale faculty who'd
been teaching in prison

00:41:25.590 --> 00:41:30.360
and who really wanted to
brainstorm, this was 2014 or 2015.

00:41:30.360 --> 00:41:33.603
How would we start a program
here? How would we do it?

00:41:35.310 --> 00:41:39.030
There had never before
been a student in prison

00:41:39.030 --> 00:41:42.540
who could say they were
part of a real Yale program.

00:41:42.540 --> 00:41:43.890
And there had never been a program

00:41:43.890 --> 00:41:45.813
that offered real Yale credits.

00:41:47.370 --> 00:41:50.637
And we also counted among us
formerly incarcerated students

00:41:50.637 --> 00:41:52.950
and graduate students on our campus

00:41:52.950 --> 00:41:55.230
who'd been alumni of
college and prison programs

00:41:55.230 --> 00:42:00.230
who were in graduate school or
who were undergraduates even.

00:42:02.250 --> 00:42:04.680
We convened a small meeting

00:42:04.680 --> 00:42:07.740
to do this imaginative work together.

00:42:07.740 --> 00:42:09.690
What would this program look like,

00:42:09.690 --> 00:42:11.163
and how would we pull it off?

00:42:13.770 --> 00:42:16.050
As an aside in working
to build this program,

00:42:16.050 --> 00:42:18.840
I spent some time considering the history

00:42:18.840 --> 00:42:21.600
of admissions at Ivy League universities.

00:42:21.600 --> 00:42:24.090
Of course, the story of inclusive changes

00:42:24.090 --> 00:42:28.290
in admissions here and
elsewhere is not ever the story

00:42:28.290 --> 00:42:32.910
of a spontaneous and benevolent increase

00:42:32.910 --> 00:42:34.950
in access to the campus,

00:42:34.950 --> 00:42:39.870
but a story of resistance by
small and subordinate groups.

00:42:39.870 --> 00:42:43.740
And that is indeed the story
of how our program was started.

00:42:43.740 --> 00:42:46.050
It was one of a fierce coalition

00:42:46.050 --> 00:42:48.870
of faculty, staff,
students, administrators,

00:42:48.870 --> 00:42:51.990
many, many administrators
and many offices,

00:42:51.990 --> 00:42:54.210
the registrar's offices who,

00:42:54.210 --> 00:42:56.010
for whatever reason, believed in it.

00:42:57.180 --> 00:43:01.110
Formally incarcerated students
as well who came to campus,

00:43:01.110 --> 00:43:03.750
attended meetings with deans at Yale

00:43:03.750 --> 00:43:06.573
and also with commissioners of correction,

00:43:08.070 --> 00:43:12.573
and who pushed until the program
finally seemed inevitable,

00:43:16.140 --> 00:43:18.417
which it still seems (chuckles).

00:43:19.560 --> 00:43:22.140
But one lesson I learned along the way

00:43:22.140 --> 00:43:25.210
that I thought this might
be a good forum to share

00:43:26.370 --> 00:43:29.520
is that it appeared to me in
this time that the university,

00:43:29.520 --> 00:43:31.510
and this is a cynical observation

00:43:32.550 --> 00:43:34.020
that maybe we can think about together,

00:43:34.020 --> 00:43:37.200
but the university is built specifically

00:43:37.200 --> 00:43:40.740
to preempt any one student or individual

00:43:40.740 --> 00:43:44.823
from creating real impact
in under four years.

00:43:45.750 --> 00:43:49.110
Four years is the amount of time it takes

00:43:49.110 --> 00:43:52.263
to create any sort of lasting change.

00:43:53.760 --> 00:43:56.970
I don't know how to subvert that sort

00:43:56.970 --> 00:44:00.480
of rule of thumb that
I've come to understand.

00:44:00.480 --> 00:44:04.140
But in those four years that
we pushed for this program,

00:44:04.140 --> 00:44:06.540
an incredible shift
happened on this campus.

00:44:06.540 --> 00:44:09.750
And many of the deans or registrars

00:44:09.750 --> 00:44:11.460
that we met with at the beginning

00:44:11.460 --> 00:44:13.357
who truly looked at me and said,

00:44:13.357 --> 00:44:16.530
"I don't physically understand
what you're talking about.

00:44:16.530 --> 00:44:19.110
Do you mean inmates are coming to campus?"

00:44:19.110 --> 00:44:19.943
And I'd say,

00:44:19.943 --> 00:44:23.610
"No, you know, faculty are gonna
drive themselves to prison,

00:44:23.610 --> 00:44:25.470
you know, and then they're
gonna be in a classroom.

00:44:25.470 --> 00:44:26.490
And in that classroom, they'll,

00:44:26.490 --> 00:44:28.770
you know, to really paint the picture.

00:44:28.770 --> 00:44:33.060
And, you know, we came
out on the other side,

00:44:33.060 --> 00:44:35.761
and some of those deans are some of,

00:44:35.761 --> 00:44:38.411
you know, the best evangelists
of this program today.

00:44:40.140 --> 00:44:43.140
So today, we have a program
that operates year-round

00:44:43.140 --> 00:44:46.470
through a partnership with
the University of New Haven.

00:44:46.470 --> 00:44:49.440
We offer both Yale and
UNH classes for credit

00:44:49.440 --> 00:44:51.840
with faculty and sometimes
graduate students

00:44:51.840 --> 00:44:54.090
from both institutions teaching.

00:44:54.090 --> 00:44:57.300
Students are matriculated
in an AA in general studies

00:44:57.300 --> 00:44:59.910
and a BA in interdisciplinary studies.

00:44:59.910 --> 00:45:02.490
If students are released before
they complete their degree,

00:45:02.490 --> 00:45:04.050
they can seamlessly transition

00:45:04.050 --> 00:45:05.940
to on-campus enrollment at UNH,

00:45:05.940 --> 00:45:08.583
which is very unique in this field.

00:45:10.110 --> 00:45:14.580
Together, we have facilitated
431 unique enrollments

00:45:14.580 --> 00:45:16.560
in credit-bearing courses

00:45:16.560 --> 00:45:19.740
with 121 faculty staff
and graduate students

00:45:19.740 --> 00:45:21.960
from both campuses
teaching, guest lecturing,

00:45:21.960 --> 00:45:23.550
or otherwise offering access

00:45:23.550 --> 00:45:26.403
to their on-campus research and teaching.

00:45:27.570 --> 00:45:32.520
And over 200 undergraduate
students have worked

00:45:32.520 --> 00:45:36.213
with us as volunteers or
tutors in various capacities.

00:45:37.380 --> 00:45:40.260
This semester, we'll
graduate our first students

00:45:40.260 --> 00:45:42.240
from the degree program.

00:45:42.240 --> 00:45:47.240
And today, students at
Danbury Federal Prison,

00:45:49.440 --> 00:45:52.623
or women, rather, at the
Danbury Federal Prison,

00:45:54.390 --> 00:45:57.510
just, you know, nearby from here,

00:45:57.510 --> 00:46:00.300
are receiving admissions letters

00:46:00.300 --> 00:46:05.253
to our first cohort for a
new program at that facility.

00:46:06.090 --> 00:46:07.260
We've also just been approved

00:46:07.260 --> 00:46:09.600
to facilitate Federal
Pell Grants in prison

00:46:09.600 --> 00:46:12.510
as a part of this national
movement that Antonne mentioned

00:46:12.510 --> 00:46:15.123
to restore Pell Grant
access for students inside.

00:46:16.710 --> 00:46:19.050
And there is a lot to be proud of,

00:46:19.050 --> 00:46:21.180
and yet also a lot that is wrong

00:46:21.180 --> 00:46:22.893
with how things have evolved.

00:46:24.210 --> 00:46:26.190
There are things that, you know,

00:46:26.190 --> 00:46:29.040
we didn't succeed at in
building this program.

00:46:29.040 --> 00:46:30.540
And there are things that I believe

00:46:30.540 --> 00:46:32.580
we still will succeed at.

00:46:32.580 --> 00:46:34.920
And a lot of this work requires

00:46:34.920 --> 00:46:36.570
a lot of imaginative thinking.

00:46:36.570 --> 00:46:39.030
And I'll get to that in just a second,

00:46:39.030 --> 00:46:41.613
but I do quickly, and then I'm gonna end.

00:46:44.859 --> 00:46:49.520
I just have several
discrete acts of imagination

00:46:50.400 --> 00:46:53.760
from our prison education program

00:46:53.760 --> 00:46:56.728
that were required to
build and to sustain it,

00:46:56.728 --> 00:47:01.020
right, in addition to
what I just mentioned,

00:47:01.020 --> 00:47:05.370
continuing to imagine the
ideal version of this program.

00:47:05.370 --> 00:47:06.990
But the first was,

00:47:06.990 --> 00:47:10.170
over those years of building
it, building a program

00:47:10.170 --> 00:47:12.780
for an imaginary group of students, right?

00:47:12.780 --> 00:47:17.130
Working with current
students in another program,

00:47:17.130 --> 00:47:19.803
knowing students from other programs,

00:47:21.090 --> 00:47:26.040
but nevertheless, fighting
through these institutions,

00:47:26.040 --> 00:47:27.990
the Department of Corrections

00:47:27.990 --> 00:47:31.110
and an institution of higher education

00:47:31.110 --> 00:47:34.740
to create a credit-bearing
or degree-granting program

00:47:34.740 --> 00:47:37.770
for a group of students that
you don't personally know.

00:47:37.770 --> 00:47:38.790
Right?

00:47:38.790 --> 00:47:41.310
And so, you know, a group of students

00:47:41.310 --> 00:47:44.088
who simply have not, you know,

00:47:44.088 --> 00:47:46.800
have not applied to the program, right?

00:47:46.800 --> 00:47:49.770
And which is why one of the best days

00:47:49.770 --> 00:47:54.770
in our lives as, you know,
prison education administrators

00:47:55.590 --> 00:47:59.580
is admitting a new cohort
of students to a prison,

00:47:59.580 --> 00:48:00.930
admitting a new cohort of students

00:48:00.930 --> 00:48:03.600
to a prison education program,
sitting down with them,

00:48:03.600 --> 00:48:05.820
and really saying, "This is for you."

00:48:05.820 --> 00:48:08.070
You know, this is being in that room

00:48:08.070 --> 00:48:09.810
and especially with our first cohorts

00:48:09.810 --> 00:48:14.810
at any prison is really, you
know, the perfect culmination

00:48:15.390 --> 00:48:17.760
of imaginative thinking.

00:48:17.760 --> 00:48:21.123
Not the perfect culmination,
a good, a fine culmination.

00:48:23.370 --> 00:48:25.500
Another act of imagination,

00:48:25.500 --> 00:48:29.700
reading a student handbook
to students in prison.

00:48:29.700 --> 00:48:33.720
It's one of the core principles
of this program is really,

00:48:33.720 --> 00:48:36.990
we're extending access to
the same college classes,

00:48:36.990 --> 00:48:41.730
the same programming that
we offer to students here.

00:48:41.730 --> 00:48:45.240
And we hold to the same
rules and regulations,

00:48:45.240 --> 00:48:46.680
we hold to the same standards,

00:48:46.680 --> 00:48:51.680
and we sit there on the first
day and read every little rule

00:48:53.070 --> 00:48:55.740
that students are trusted to read

00:48:55.740 --> 00:48:59.460
on their own here on campus.

00:48:59.460 --> 00:49:04.417
And reading that student
handbook is a little bit odd,

00:49:05.460 --> 00:49:09.240
because some of these
rules simply don't apply.

00:49:09.240 --> 00:49:14.240
But the work that it does
is in helping the students

00:49:15.510 --> 00:49:18.123
to understand themselves as students,

00:49:19.410 --> 00:49:21.360
as peers to students on our campus,

00:49:21.360 --> 00:49:26.360
that they are bound to those
same ways of being a student.

00:49:28.590 --> 00:49:32.923
A third act of imagination
is the way we constantly use

00:49:35.160 --> 00:49:38.400
and reimagine the Yale mission statement.

00:49:38.400 --> 00:49:40.920
We use, as our mission statement for YPEI,

00:49:40.920 --> 00:49:42.750
the mission statement of Yale College,

00:49:42.750 --> 00:49:46.200
to seek exceptionally promising
students of all backgrounds

00:49:46.200 --> 00:49:48.780
from across the nation
and around the world,

00:49:48.780 --> 00:49:50.850
and to educate them
through mental discipline

00:49:50.850 --> 00:49:54.180
and social experience, to
develop their intellectual,

00:49:54.180 --> 00:49:57.300
moral, civic, and creative
capacities to the fullest.

00:49:57.300 --> 00:49:59.580
The aim of this education
is the cultivation

00:49:59.580 --> 00:50:02.190
of citizens with a rich
awareness of our heritage

00:50:02.190 --> 00:50:05.583
to lead and serve in every
sphere of human activity.

00:50:06.720 --> 00:50:08.590
So we take this mission statement

00:50:09.870 --> 00:50:13.983
and we read it every day
through an abolitionist lens.

00:50:14.880 --> 00:50:17.050
And the way we do that is we imagine

00:50:18.960 --> 00:50:23.790
that if we can't imagine
together a post-carceral future,

00:50:23.790 --> 00:50:25.830
then the leaders of that future

00:50:25.830 --> 00:50:30.180
may very well be incarcerated
today, are incarcerated today.

00:50:30.180 --> 00:50:32.640
And in that scenario, the
charge of the university

00:50:32.640 --> 00:50:36.030
is to invest now in those students

00:50:36.030 --> 00:50:38.340
as future leaders and citizens.

00:50:38.340 --> 00:50:39.870
So you don't have to change anything

00:50:39.870 --> 00:50:43.260
about the mission statement
to read it through that lens,

00:50:43.260 --> 00:50:46.530
but it is a way that we have to work

00:50:46.530 --> 00:50:48.873
to imagine it serving a different purpose.

00:50:51.300 --> 00:50:54.340
My fourth act of imagination

00:50:56.430 --> 00:51:00.270
is a little anecdote, and
then I'll cut myself off,

00:51:00.270 --> 00:51:02.220
'cause I have many acts of imagination.

00:51:03.228 --> 00:51:04.230
(audience chuckling)

00:51:04.230 --> 00:51:08.820
Creating Yale within the prison

00:51:08.820 --> 00:51:11.610
is an interesting practice for us.

00:51:11.610 --> 00:51:15.270
If you can imagine, our
faculty, our volunteers,

00:51:15.270 --> 00:51:20.270
and our staff are a certain
subset of Yale citizens.

00:51:21.180 --> 00:51:26.180
And we, you know, we bring
in a lot of, you know,

00:51:28.470 --> 00:51:31.290
a lot of the faculty that you see here.

00:51:31.290 --> 00:51:33.720
And there's a lot of
undergraduates who volunteer

00:51:33.720 --> 00:51:36.780
who are students of color or
who are radical activists,

00:51:36.780 --> 00:51:39.603
who believe, you know,
there's a lot of community

00:51:43.710 --> 00:51:45.963
in the people who enter the prison.

00:51:47.820 --> 00:51:51.960
And I remember one day a couple years ago,

00:51:51.960 --> 00:51:54.420
we had a sort of, as Peter mentioned,

00:51:54.420 --> 00:51:56.553
I went to undergrad and grad school here,

00:51:57.480 --> 00:51:59.880
and we had a student who was volunteering

00:51:59.880 --> 00:52:02.010
who maybe more fit you traditionally,

00:52:02.010 --> 00:52:04.440
he was like a little very
tall lacrosse player,

00:52:04.440 --> 00:52:06.630
very white and blonde.

00:52:06.630 --> 00:52:08.370
And he came into the prison,

00:52:08.370 --> 00:52:12.120
and, you know, our students
are mostly black and brown

00:52:12.120 --> 00:52:16.170
from, you know, neighborhoods
in Connecticut cities.

00:52:16.170 --> 00:52:21.170
And they looked at this guy and they said,

00:52:21.853 --> 00:52:25.855
"He doesn't look like
a Yale student to me."

00:52:25.855 --> 00:52:27.304
(audience laughing)

00:52:27.304 --> 00:52:31.463
And I was like, "Yeah, we've
created like an ERNM college

00:52:32.722 --> 00:52:34.620
(audience laughing)

00:52:34.620 --> 00:52:37.530
at McDougal Walker
Correctional Institution."

00:52:37.530 --> 00:52:40.170
And it was a really strange feeling,

00:52:40.170 --> 00:52:42.810
'cause especially, a couple of
years ago when this happened,

00:52:42.810 --> 00:52:45.600
we were really, there
wasn't very much interest

00:52:45.600 --> 00:52:47.550
in teaching or learning in the program.

00:52:47.550 --> 00:52:50.250
We had, you know, some interest,

00:52:50.250 --> 00:52:52.230
but that semester, we had Rod Ferguson

00:52:52.230 --> 00:52:54.591
and Lisa Lowe and Dan HoSang

00:52:54.591 --> 00:52:57.090
and Liam Irakork coteaching ERNM

00:52:57.090 --> 00:52:59.223
because that was all we could do.

00:53:00.090 --> 00:53:04.890
And so, you know, having
that kind of reality check

00:53:04.890 --> 00:53:08.310
was really, it was a weird moment,

00:53:11.280 --> 00:53:13.110
but it also helped me think

00:53:13.110 --> 00:53:16.230
about how we have created
this version of Yale.

00:53:16.230 --> 00:53:18.930
We've imagined this version
of Yale within the prison.

00:53:21.810 --> 00:53:25.140
Okay, and I'm gonna end with
just a little reflection

00:53:25.140 --> 00:53:28.408
and something maybe
slightly uncomfortable.

00:53:28.408 --> 00:53:32.250
I'm super grateful to be
sitting here next to Antonne.

00:53:32.250 --> 00:53:35.820
There's something a little bit
strange about being at this,

00:53:35.820 --> 00:53:36.930
I don't have an answer to this.

00:53:36.930 --> 00:53:38.370
This is just my notes.

00:53:38.370 --> 00:53:39.600
A little bit strange about being

00:53:39.600 --> 00:53:42.120
at a symposium with scholars and writers,

00:53:42.120 --> 00:53:44.940
and everyone's got a
friend in prison, you know,

00:53:44.940 --> 00:53:46.380
or a person they write letters

00:53:46.380 --> 00:53:50.460
to or about or who they teach or refer to.

00:53:50.460 --> 00:53:54.063
It's a little bit itchy.
It's a little bit itchy.

00:53:55.050 --> 00:53:56.940
But it also does this
really important work

00:53:56.940 --> 00:53:59.670
we were talking earlier about
making the invisible visible,

00:53:59.670 --> 00:54:01.860
bringing to the forefront
of discussion people

00:54:01.860 --> 00:54:03.780
who are hidden from view,

00:54:03.780 --> 00:54:06.963
people actively, currently in prison.

00:54:09.360 --> 00:54:11.223
And am I out of time?

00:54:13.443 --> 00:54:15.797
- Just us, you know, so.
- Yeah, yeah, okay.

00:54:15.797 --> 00:54:19.620
I just had like a little paragraph,

00:54:19.620 --> 00:54:24.060
but a little bit off topic,

00:54:24.060 --> 00:54:26.460
but my background is in film studies.

00:54:26.460 --> 00:54:27.900
And one thing I've written about

00:54:27.900 --> 00:54:30.390
is background actors
in classical Hollywood.

00:54:30.390 --> 00:54:31.740
So in the '20s and '30s,

00:54:31.740 --> 00:54:34.290
there were these cycles of prison films

00:54:34.290 --> 00:54:37.560
that fetishized using
actual prison locations,

00:54:37.560 --> 00:54:38.910
often with the permission

00:54:38.910 --> 00:54:43.050
of like a very enthusiastic
Hollywood-hungry warden.

00:54:43.050 --> 00:54:46.470
And then beyond that, they
would use actual prisoners

00:54:46.470 --> 00:54:48.780
in the backgrounds of the films.

00:54:48.780 --> 00:54:51.390
And in most of these scenarios,

00:54:51.390 --> 00:54:53.700
it was required by the prison

00:54:53.700 --> 00:54:55.290
and by the motion picture industry

00:54:55.290 --> 00:54:58.320
that the background actors
could not be portrayed

00:54:58.320 --> 00:55:00.270
in any degree of specificity

00:55:00.270 --> 00:55:02.493
lest they require fair compensation.

00:55:03.360 --> 00:55:05.320
So sometimes, in the backgrounds

00:55:07.560 --> 00:55:09.930
of films that would take place in prison,

00:55:09.930 --> 00:55:14.010
you'd see people who were incarcerated,

00:55:14.010 --> 00:55:15.960
but they were masked in order

00:55:15.960 --> 00:55:19.413
to suppress their identity,
their portrait, you see.

00:55:20.910 --> 00:55:22.590
And I wasn't even gonna talk about this,

00:55:22.590 --> 00:55:24.400
but after the rest of the,

00:55:24.400 --> 00:55:26.070
I was sort of thinking about it,

00:55:26.070 --> 00:55:27.177
and I'm thinking about this history

00:55:27.177 --> 00:55:30.750
and the work that people are doing

00:55:30.750 --> 00:55:35.750
in accompanying portraits of
individuals on the inside,

00:55:35.910 --> 00:55:39.303
making this sort of invisible visible.

00:55:41.670 --> 00:55:45.003
And yeah, that's it. I'll end there.

00:55:46.594 --> 00:55:47.519
Thanks.

00:55:47.519 --> 00:55:50.686
(audience applauding)

00:55:52.140 --> 00:55:52.973
- Thanks, Zelda.

00:55:52.973 --> 00:55:54.570
I wanna leave some time, if I can,

00:55:54.570 --> 00:55:56.940
too for some questions from the audience.

00:55:56.940 --> 00:55:59.100
I'm sure after these
terrific presentations,

00:55:59.100 --> 00:56:00.900
there will be some.

00:56:00.900 --> 00:56:04.350
So I'm Ben Berger, Swarthmore College.

00:56:04.350 --> 00:56:07.890
Like Tracey Meares before
me, I'm gonna try to respond

00:56:07.890 --> 00:56:11.220
not just to the terrific
presentations we've had here,

00:56:11.220 --> 00:56:12.720
but also to some of the things

00:56:12.720 --> 00:56:14.220
that have been said earlier in the day

00:56:14.220 --> 00:56:17.130
and even from at the dinner
last night for presenters

00:56:17.130 --> 00:56:18.870
to try to give, maybe,
a little bit of a sense

00:56:18.870 --> 00:56:21.480
of all the things that
tie, have tied us together.

00:56:21.480 --> 00:56:23.190
I'm really grateful,
as others are as well,

00:56:23.190 --> 00:56:25.650
to Peter for bringing us together.

00:56:25.650 --> 00:56:28.980
And there's this kind of simpatico
on this particular panel.

00:56:28.980 --> 00:56:31.530
I met Peter when I was at Princeton.

00:56:31.530 --> 00:56:33.390
I was the Rockefeller visiting professor

00:56:33.390 --> 00:56:34.830
for distinguished teaching there,

00:56:34.830 --> 00:56:37.200
and he had been the same
thing a few years before.

00:56:37.200 --> 00:56:39.030
We connected through that.

00:56:39.030 --> 00:56:41.340
I was teaching a class,
an Inside-Out class,

00:56:41.340 --> 00:56:42.900
that is said brought Princeton students

00:56:42.900 --> 00:56:44.970
into East Jersey State Prison,

00:56:44.970 --> 00:56:48.570
and they took a class alongside
the incarcerated students.

00:56:48.570 --> 00:56:50.190
That was something that Peter

00:56:50.190 --> 00:56:51.450
was interested in doing as well.

00:56:51.450 --> 00:56:52.470
We talked a lot about that.

00:56:52.470 --> 00:56:54.030
He had taught at EGSP,

00:56:54.030 --> 00:56:56.940
he had taught Tone, who
had come home already,

00:56:56.940 --> 00:56:59.820
and Tone became my guide to figure out.

00:56:59.820 --> 00:57:01.770
I was experienced at prison teaching,

00:57:01.770 --> 00:57:03.420
but every institution is different,

00:57:03.420 --> 00:57:05.820
and you've gotta figure
out what to do where.

00:57:05.820 --> 00:57:07.830
and the kind of advice I got

00:57:07.830 --> 00:57:09.270
from Tone was just invaluable.

00:57:09.270 --> 00:57:10.920
And it wasn't just at the
beginning of the semester,

00:57:10.920 --> 00:57:12.470
it was throughout the semester.

00:57:13.680 --> 00:57:14.910
Bernard and I have talked about

00:57:14.910 --> 00:57:16.350
and know a lot of people in common

00:57:16.350 --> 00:57:17.880
because we've gone to
some of the same schools.

00:57:17.880 --> 00:57:19.440
He's just done a lot more than I have,

00:57:19.440 --> 00:57:21.000
but I don't hold that against him.

00:57:21.000 --> 00:57:23.520
And his book "Critique
and Praxis" is fabulous,

00:57:23.520 --> 00:57:24.450
and you should get it,

00:57:24.450 --> 00:57:26.070
because it's informed my thinking

00:57:26.070 --> 00:57:28.140
about some of what we're
talking about today.

00:57:28.140 --> 00:57:29.160
It's a recent book,

00:57:29.160 --> 00:57:31.560
and it's not just if you wanna
understand critical theory,

00:57:31.560 --> 00:57:33.150
but also if you really wanna understand,

00:57:33.150 --> 00:57:35.250
actually, what a critical theorist

00:57:35.250 --> 00:57:37.950
and one who's also an activist might do

00:57:37.950 --> 00:57:39.570
in pushing back against himself,

00:57:39.570 --> 00:57:41.340
always questioning, am I doing enough?

00:57:41.340 --> 00:57:42.600
Am I doing the right thing?

00:57:42.600 --> 00:57:45.420
Which is something that all of
us ought to be doing really.

00:57:45.420 --> 00:57:47.610
The overarching theme that I wanna talk,

00:57:47.610 --> 00:57:50.070
bring together is the one of imagination.

00:57:50.070 --> 00:57:52.290
Bernard reminds us with the example

00:57:52.290 --> 00:57:54.240
of reading groups in the carceral spaces.

00:57:54.240 --> 00:57:57.540
Antonne reinforces with
his really moving examples

00:57:57.540 --> 00:58:01.350
of them that things don't happen.

00:58:01.350 --> 00:58:04.860
We don't build things unless
we can imagine them, right?

00:58:04.860 --> 00:58:06.990
Unless they already happen
to us in our experience

00:58:06.990 --> 00:58:07.890
or if we can imagine them.

00:58:07.890 --> 00:58:09.690
And books are one way of doing that.

00:58:09.690 --> 00:58:10.890
And not just books,

00:58:10.890 --> 00:58:14.130
but then talking about books
with other people as well.

00:58:14.130 --> 00:58:18.420
So that's the basic power of imagination

00:58:18.420 --> 00:58:21.630
to help us to figure out what
we might be able to build,

00:58:21.630 --> 00:58:24.000
that just can't be underestimated.

00:58:24.000 --> 00:58:26.550
I wanna recount a few
things from other panels

00:58:26.550 --> 00:58:28.890
to set the stage for what
we are talking about here.

00:58:28.890 --> 00:58:30.720
Joy James, this morning, wondered

00:58:30.720 --> 00:58:34.530
about the protective mechanism
that authors of fiction

00:58:34.530 --> 00:58:36.420
might be able to offer the incarcerated

00:58:36.420 --> 00:58:38.250
if some incarcerated people

00:58:38.250 --> 00:58:41.640
have difficulty being seen by the public.

00:58:41.640 --> 00:58:43.740
Now Caleb pointed out
that there are some truths

00:58:43.740 --> 00:58:47.610
that people in power don't
want incarcerated writers

00:58:47.610 --> 00:58:50.940
to tell or other writers to
tell about the incarcerated.

00:58:50.940 --> 00:58:51.960
There's a double bind.

00:58:51.960 --> 00:58:54.930
You wanna say the right
thing but not say too much

00:58:54.930 --> 00:58:57.720
and not get in trouble
with the powers that be.

00:58:57.720 --> 00:58:59.550
And Rachel Kushner shared with us

00:58:59.550 --> 00:59:02.400
that story of the poetry editor

00:59:02.400 --> 00:59:04.200
that said for a prisoner's bio,

00:59:04.200 --> 00:59:06.420
they could include only the person's name,

00:59:06.420 --> 00:59:08.280
age, where they were incarcerated,

00:59:08.280 --> 00:59:09.810
and the crime for which
they were convicted.

00:59:09.810 --> 00:59:12.480
That was the bio. Nothing
else about the person.

00:59:12.480 --> 00:59:15.600
There was a kind of
vanishing, a kind of erasure.

00:59:15.600 --> 00:59:18.510
A strict control over the resources

00:59:18.510 --> 00:59:21.000
that readers might be able to access

00:59:21.000 --> 00:59:23.430
when they create the film in their mind

00:59:23.430 --> 00:59:25.980
of thinking about life for
that incarcerated person.

00:59:25.980 --> 00:59:29.130
And I think most of us agree
that's not an accident.

00:59:29.130 --> 00:59:30.990
It's not an accident.
There is strict control.

00:59:30.990 --> 00:59:33.750
Telling the story you told
about the the parole officer

00:59:33.750 --> 00:59:36.480
even outside the prison and
the control that's exercised,

00:59:36.480 --> 00:59:38.370
it's not an accident.

00:59:38.370 --> 00:59:40.650
One of the things that
struck me most forcefully

00:59:40.650 --> 00:59:42.540
when I started doing prison teaching

00:59:42.540 --> 00:59:46.410
was this intentional erasure
of bodies and personalities,

00:59:46.410 --> 00:59:49.200
a vanishing of people, not
just from the broader public,

00:59:49.200 --> 00:59:51.000
but sometimes even from families as well,

00:59:51.000 --> 00:59:53.460
the inability to bring pictures out.

00:59:53.460 --> 00:59:56.250
My group at Princeton,
we took a class picture.

00:59:56.250 --> 00:59:58.500
We had somebody from one
of the photography groups

00:59:58.500 --> 01:00:00.450
coming in and take a picture of us.

01:00:00.450 --> 01:00:01.350
I smuggled that out.

01:00:01.350 --> 01:00:02.820
I wasn't supposed to have
it get out of the prison.

01:00:02.820 --> 01:00:03.660
It seems ridiculous.

01:00:03.660 --> 01:00:06.330
Why shouldn't we have
a picture of us there?

01:00:06.330 --> 01:00:07.680
But we weren't, all right?

01:00:07.680 --> 01:00:10.530
Even images weren't supposed to go out.

01:00:10.530 --> 01:00:13.380
And so it's one of the
reasons why I appreciate

01:00:13.380 --> 01:00:15.720
not only prison education
programs in general,

01:00:15.720 --> 01:00:17.070
like Zelda, the one you've created here

01:00:17.070 --> 01:00:19.260
and my hat is off to you,

01:00:19.260 --> 01:00:21.420
but especially the Inside-Out model

01:00:21.420 --> 01:00:23.370
that was created at Graterford Prison,

01:00:23.370 --> 01:00:25.830
now called the Phoenix
and Temple University.

01:00:25.830 --> 01:00:27.390
So I practice the Inside-Out model,

01:00:27.390 --> 01:00:29.160
people all over the world do it as well.

01:00:29.160 --> 01:00:31.830
That is where, like at
Princeton that one year, right?

01:00:31.830 --> 01:00:35.250
You have a class inside
the carceral facility.

01:00:35.250 --> 01:00:37.710
And there are students
from your home institution,

01:00:37.710 --> 01:00:39.210
they're the outside students

01:00:39.210 --> 01:00:40.680
together with the incarcerated students,

01:00:40.680 --> 01:00:42.240
they're the inside students,

01:00:42.240 --> 01:00:43.830
and you're reading the same books,

01:00:43.830 --> 01:00:45.030
you're writing the same papers,

01:00:45.030 --> 01:00:47.310
having the same discussions,
they are transformative.

01:00:47.310 --> 01:00:50.790
But what they also do is
they allow outside students

01:00:50.790 --> 01:00:53.790
to go home and realize
and tell other people too,

01:00:53.790 --> 01:00:56.520
this is the kind of erasure, I
saw what's actually going on,

01:00:56.520 --> 01:00:58.350
and it's not what's
being portrayed out here.

01:00:58.350 --> 01:00:59.580
So it's one way in which,

01:00:59.580 --> 01:01:02.250
a little way in which their imaginations

01:01:02.250 --> 01:01:04.770
and what they convey to
others can be expanded

01:01:04.770 --> 01:01:07.020
in terms of possibly
changing things as well.

01:01:08.160 --> 01:01:10.350
Zelda, this great work you've
done, again, here with Yale.

01:01:10.350 --> 01:01:12.300
We're trying around Swarthmore

01:01:12.300 --> 01:01:14.670
and in Pennsylvania more
broadly to create our own kind

01:01:14.670 --> 01:01:16.680
of prison education
system, we're well filipa.

01:01:16.680 --> 01:01:18.900
I mean, Villanova's got
its own great program,

01:01:18.900 --> 01:01:20.730
but PA doesn't, we're trying to kind

01:01:20.730 --> 01:01:23.070
of copy what's in New Jersey.

01:01:23.070 --> 01:01:25.800
I learned so much about
NJ-STEP through Ton,

01:01:25.800 --> 01:01:28.770
at where there is one central
school, that's Rutgers,

01:01:28.770 --> 01:01:31.380
that grants the BAs no
matter who at what school,

01:01:31.380 --> 01:01:35.100
Drew, Princeton, wherever,
might teach a class inside,

01:01:35.100 --> 01:01:37.620
but Rutgers is the
BA-granting institution.

01:01:37.620 --> 01:01:39.270
And then Raritan Valley Community College

01:01:39.270 --> 01:01:41.010
is the AA-granting institution.

01:01:41.010 --> 01:01:42.180
We're trying to do something

01:01:42.180 --> 01:01:43.860
like that with Penn State as the main one,

01:01:43.860 --> 01:01:45.540
so that when I teach a course inside,

01:01:45.540 --> 01:01:49.770
my students have credit
towards a Penn State degree

01:01:49.770 --> 01:01:51.270
or a community college degree.

01:01:52.230 --> 01:01:53.063
But here's the thing,

01:01:53.063 --> 01:01:56.010
and I wanna raise this to
Bernard and also to Zelda.

01:01:56.010 --> 01:01:57.390
This is not really a critique.

01:01:57.390 --> 01:01:58.800
It's where I wanna lift something

01:01:58.800 --> 01:02:02.400
up as kind of an
expansion, not a critique.

01:02:02.400 --> 01:02:04.620
And it came up last night at dinner

01:02:04.620 --> 01:02:05.910
with Rich Rivera and Tone.

01:02:05.910 --> 01:02:09.780
We had a fabulous conversation
about inside life,

01:02:09.780 --> 01:02:11.310
but also coming home and reentry

01:02:11.310 --> 01:02:13.023
and about parole boards as well.

01:02:13.890 --> 01:02:16.200
And Rich made the really interesting point

01:02:16.200 --> 01:02:18.240
that, I mean, shouldn't
come as a surprise to us,

01:02:18.240 --> 01:02:19.680
but sometimes you gotta
gotta have things brought

01:02:19.680 --> 01:02:22.230
to the level of consciousness
to really deal with them,

01:02:22.230 --> 01:02:25.350
that, of course, we don't
want to just hold everybody

01:02:25.350 --> 01:02:27.000
inside before they come home

01:02:27.000 --> 01:02:28.680
or before they're considered a success

01:02:28.680 --> 01:02:30.865
that they are like Tone or Rich.

01:02:30.865 --> 01:02:32.700
People you've met who have come home

01:02:32.700 --> 01:02:35.730
often are just amazing people.

01:02:35.730 --> 01:02:38.070
They would've succeeded
anywhere in the world.

01:02:38.070 --> 01:02:39.390
They're people who had the drive

01:02:39.390 --> 01:02:40.620
to go through a college degree,

01:02:40.620 --> 01:02:43.020
to become paralegals, to
do all kinds of stuff.

01:02:43.020 --> 01:02:44.790
But there are many paths to success,

01:02:44.790 --> 01:02:48.780
and it doesn't have to come
necessarily just through books,

01:02:48.780 --> 01:02:49.920
a particular kind of book,

01:02:49.920 --> 01:02:51.270
a particular kind of discussion group.

01:02:51.270 --> 01:02:52.200
That doesn't invalidate.

01:02:52.200 --> 01:02:53.490
I mean, we are dedicated to that.

01:02:53.490 --> 01:02:55.890
We're intellectuals, we're academics.

01:02:55.890 --> 01:02:59.910
Rich pointed out that if you
play basketball for 20 years,

01:02:59.910 --> 01:03:00.930
you're dedicated to that.

01:03:00.930 --> 01:03:03.270
It takes more than we can recognize

01:03:03.270 --> 01:03:04.980
because it's a scarce resource.

01:03:04.980 --> 01:03:06.810
Courts are a scarce resource.

01:03:06.810 --> 01:03:10.980
Being able to stay dedicated,
focused, stay out of trouble,

01:03:10.980 --> 01:03:12.510
the many different things you've gotta do

01:03:12.510 --> 01:03:14.670
to be able to do that for 20, 25 years,

01:03:14.670 --> 01:03:16.620
that's stuff that will
work on the outside too.

01:03:16.620 --> 01:03:18.720
That's stuff that needs
to be recognized as well.

01:03:18.720 --> 01:03:20.670
So it may be one thing that some

01:03:20.670 --> 01:03:23.160
of us here can try to lift
up to the broader public

01:03:23.160 --> 01:03:24.997
who can put pressure on
parole boards to say,

01:03:24.997 --> 01:03:26.490
"Well, it's not just one way.

01:03:26.490 --> 01:03:28.950
You don't have to be someone
who's got a perfect GPA

01:03:28.950 --> 01:03:31.350
or something to be able to come home."

01:03:31.350 --> 01:03:33.390
Again, that's an
expansion, not a critique,

01:03:33.390 --> 01:03:36.507
I think, of what you
guys are working towards.

01:03:36.507 --> 01:03:39.030
And Zelda, I was asking
Zelda this before this,

01:03:39.030 --> 01:03:40.290
but I'd love to talk afterwards

01:03:40.290 --> 01:03:43.380
too about the possibility
of bridge programs

01:03:43.380 --> 01:03:45.420
for folks who aren't ready right away

01:03:45.420 --> 01:03:48.900
to come in and start a college
program inside, but want to.

01:03:48.900 --> 01:03:50.160
Having tutors in place,

01:03:50.160 --> 01:03:52.320
having Yale tutors in place
or wherever the school

01:03:52.320 --> 01:03:54.060
is to help get them to the level

01:03:54.060 --> 01:03:56.190
where now you're ready
to start, all right?

01:03:56.190 --> 01:03:58.867
And so we're not only
dealing with people who say,

01:03:58.867 --> 01:04:02.190
"I wanna read Mao right now.
I wanna read Marx right now."

01:04:02.190 --> 01:04:03.378
Right?

01:04:03.378 --> 01:04:04.277
So there's lots of paths

01:04:04.277 --> 01:04:06.573
to that kind of success I'm drawn to.

01:04:07.470 --> 01:04:10.530
Let me just say one thing
about imagination broadly.

01:04:10.530 --> 01:04:11.550
It was the theme of today.

01:04:11.550 --> 01:04:14.611
So we talked about, like, visibility,

01:04:14.611 --> 01:04:17.250
and Tracey gave a great
little sort of pushback

01:04:17.250 --> 01:04:19.590
about visibility, well,
maybe that's not enough,

01:04:19.590 --> 01:04:21.270
'cause some things are visible,

01:04:21.270 --> 01:04:23.310
but they don't necessarily lead to change.

01:04:23.310 --> 01:04:26.760
We may ignore them, we may
push back against them.

01:04:26.760 --> 01:04:29.730
So what I wanna specify with imagination

01:04:29.730 --> 01:04:33.330
is that imagination needs
building blocks, it needs food,

01:04:33.330 --> 01:04:35.700
not food for thought,
but food for imagination.

01:04:35.700 --> 01:04:37.830
So I think a little bit of Thomas Hobbes,

01:04:37.830 --> 01:04:39.840
you don't have to like Hobbes'
overall political theory

01:04:39.840 --> 01:04:43.410
or even endorse his adamistic
approach to the world

01:04:43.410 --> 01:04:44.910
to like this, what I'm
gonna put out there.

01:04:44.910 --> 01:04:45.743
He says that,

01:04:45.743 --> 01:04:49.050
"Imagination, it's
particles. It's particulate."

01:04:49.050 --> 01:04:54.004
So since the world meets us
here through our experiences,

01:04:54.004 --> 01:04:57.720
our sense organs process
that, and then afterwards,

01:04:57.720 --> 01:05:00.360
the kind of echoes of that,
they become available to us.

01:05:00.360 --> 01:05:02.850
These faint imprints as imagination.

01:05:02.850 --> 01:05:04.770
But it's only things
we've encountered already.

01:05:04.770 --> 01:05:07.350
You can't imagine something
you had no idea about.

01:05:07.350 --> 01:05:09.060
You might encounter it
through somebody else telling

01:05:09.060 --> 01:05:11.160
you about it or through your experience.

01:05:11.160 --> 01:05:14.610
We need food, we need particles
to feed the imagination.

01:05:14.610 --> 01:05:18.000
And Zach said something
earlier about novelists,

01:05:18.000 --> 01:05:21.240
possibly being able to order and organize

01:05:21.240 --> 01:05:23.820
the particles that the
rest of the world sees.

01:05:23.820 --> 01:05:25.620
That's what I wanna put out there.

01:05:25.620 --> 01:05:27.630
There's lots of
information that could come

01:05:27.630 --> 01:05:30.840
out to make things less
invisible about prisons.

01:05:30.840 --> 01:05:32.700
But we need people who are gonna be able

01:05:32.700 --> 01:05:35.070
to organize the particles into something

01:05:35.070 --> 01:05:37.500
then they can expand a popular imagination

01:05:37.500 --> 01:05:39.000
about what's actually happening

01:05:39.000 --> 01:05:40.920
and what could happen as well.

01:05:40.920 --> 01:05:43.320
So I heard a lot of
sub-themes like that today.

01:05:43.320 --> 01:05:44.610
I just wanna kind of lift that up.

01:05:44.610 --> 01:05:49.110
I hear that in today's panelists as well.

01:05:49.110 --> 01:05:52.830
To build, to rebuild, to reform,
reboot, to revolutionize,

01:05:52.830 --> 01:05:54.000
we have to be able to imagine it.

01:05:54.000 --> 01:05:56.190
Now, just 'cause you can imagine something

01:05:56.190 --> 01:05:57.810
doesn't mean that it's possible.

01:05:57.810 --> 01:05:59.490
You can imagine flying pigs

01:05:59.490 --> 01:06:01.410
and maybe genetic engineering will,

01:06:01.410 --> 01:06:03.035
I don't know what that's
gonna come up with,

01:06:03.035 --> 01:06:04.260
but right now they're not possible.

01:06:04.260 --> 01:06:07.380
So it may not be sufficient,
but it's necessary.

01:06:07.380 --> 01:06:09.210
You gotta be able to imagine it.

01:06:09.210 --> 01:06:10.960
And that's what we've gotta expand.

01:06:13.080 --> 01:06:15.570
So let me see, I see
only a couple more things

01:06:15.570 --> 01:06:17.100
so I can get to some other questions

01:06:17.100 --> 01:06:20.610
and comments on the panelists, right.

01:06:20.610 --> 01:06:23.520
Ton, when we go to
questions and conversation,

01:06:23.520 --> 01:06:26.130
I'd love to invite you
to expand on something

01:06:26.130 --> 01:06:27.360
that we've talked about before,

01:06:27.360 --> 01:06:29.010
and you've also talked about with Bernard,

01:06:29.010 --> 01:06:31.050
with Zelda, with Peter
on different occasions

01:06:31.050 --> 01:06:33.930
about a way of expanding imagination

01:06:33.930 --> 01:06:35.370
through changing the rhetoric,

01:06:35.370 --> 01:06:39.120
and no longer talking
perhaps about just crime,

01:06:39.120 --> 01:06:40.830
but about public health,
the public health model.

01:06:40.830 --> 01:06:44.040
I'd love to invite you to
do that once I wrap up here.

01:06:44.040 --> 01:06:46.470
I think it's really deep,
and it shows the importance

01:06:46.470 --> 01:06:49.110
of rhetoric to organize the particles,

01:06:49.110 --> 01:06:50.580
to be able to get people on board.

01:06:50.580 --> 01:06:52.380
Because here's the other thing.

01:06:52.380 --> 01:06:53.880
I think we're not, often,

01:06:53.880 --> 01:06:55.200
folks here are probably pretty much

01:06:55.200 --> 01:06:57.270
on the same page when it comes to a lot

01:06:57.270 --> 01:06:59.400
of issues regarding the carceral state,

01:06:59.400 --> 01:07:01.080
to a lot of issues regarding injustice

01:07:01.080 --> 01:07:02.250
and what needs to change.

01:07:02.250 --> 01:07:04.860
But it's a big country,
and not everybody agrees.

01:07:04.860 --> 01:07:08.310
And we've seen some horrifying
examples of polarization,

01:07:08.310 --> 01:07:11.850
and yet you need other people,
unless you just wanna think

01:07:11.850 --> 01:07:13.980
that a very small band are
gonna change everything,

01:07:13.980 --> 01:07:15.447
and we saw that kind of on January 6th,

01:07:15.447 --> 01:07:17.550
and it thankfully didn't work out.

01:07:17.550 --> 01:07:19.230
But unless you wanna go that path,

01:07:19.230 --> 01:07:20.820
you need a lot of people to be brought in.

01:07:20.820 --> 01:07:22.350
And so you may need to put things

01:07:22.350 --> 01:07:24.000
in other languages as well.

01:07:24.000 --> 01:07:27.420
I sometimes wonder if
it wouldn't be better

01:07:27.420 --> 01:07:31.380
to have something like a
situation comedy written

01:07:31.380 --> 01:07:34.320
by incarcerated folks but at
many different institutions

01:07:34.320 --> 01:07:35.430
so that the stories they're talking

01:07:35.430 --> 01:07:38.610
about couldn't be recognizable
all coming from one place.

01:07:38.610 --> 01:07:39.570
But their stories,

01:07:39.570 --> 01:07:43.140
real stories within
characters who are humanized,

01:07:43.140 --> 01:07:45.930
for lack of a better word,
to the larger population.

01:07:45.930 --> 01:07:49.800
Sitcoms and popular culture,
music also, hiphop music,

01:07:49.800 --> 01:07:51.600
those things get through
to a lot of people

01:07:51.600 --> 01:07:53.640
in the way that languages often don't,

01:07:53.640 --> 01:07:55.650
and can create the
atmosphere for social change.

01:07:55.650 --> 01:07:57.810
So I don't care if it's a
sitcom or what the thing is.

01:07:57.810 --> 01:07:59.760
I just mean stories that go out there

01:07:59.760 --> 01:08:02.580
that aren't just accusatory
and that actually say,

01:08:02.580 --> 01:08:05.100
actually, we, a lot of us,
share the same things here.

01:08:05.100 --> 01:08:07.830
A lot of us know people who are in prison.

01:08:07.830 --> 01:08:08.820
A lot of us have relatives,

01:08:08.820 --> 01:08:10.680
whether we are living in
this part of the country,

01:08:10.680 --> 01:08:12.960
that part of the country,
we're all hurt by this.

01:08:12.960 --> 01:08:14.490
Our communities are hurt when people

01:08:14.490 --> 01:08:16.620
are taken out of the the workforce.

01:08:16.620 --> 01:08:19.110
Our families are hurt
when parents are taken

01:08:19.110 --> 01:08:21.480
out of the parenting environment.

01:08:21.480 --> 01:08:23.100
And that's something that we all share.

01:08:23.100 --> 01:08:25.470
So I wanna advice to think about,

01:08:25.470 --> 01:08:28.440
together, different ways
beyond also just the academic,

01:08:28.440 --> 01:08:30.783
although I am an academic, of course.

01:08:33.675 --> 01:08:37.020
All right, last thing,
engaged scholarship.

01:08:37.020 --> 01:08:39.030
Peter mentioned that I direct
the center at Swarthmore.

01:08:39.030 --> 01:08:41.310
Its focus is engaged scholarship.

01:08:41.310 --> 01:08:42.720
It used to be community-based learning.

01:08:42.720 --> 01:08:43.553
When I came in,

01:08:43.553 --> 01:08:45.900
we changed it to engaged
scholarship because it's broader.

01:08:45.900 --> 01:08:47.250
It lets a lot more faculty

01:08:47.250 --> 01:08:49.830
in who are doing maybe
things on public policy,

01:08:49.830 --> 01:08:52.110
maybe things that aren't
just about working

01:08:52.110 --> 01:08:54.000
in a particular local community.

01:08:54.000 --> 01:08:55.863
Ernest Boyer coined that term.

01:08:56.880 --> 01:08:58.920
What he meant by it
was engaged scholarship

01:08:58.920 --> 01:09:02.670
is turning, orienting the
resources of the university

01:09:02.670 --> 01:09:04.830
towards our most pressing public problems,

01:09:04.830 --> 01:09:07.680
issues, ethical issues,
civic issues, et cetera.

01:09:07.680 --> 01:09:10.050
However, what Boyer missed was this.

01:09:10.050 --> 01:09:11.520
It's more than that now.

01:09:11.520 --> 01:09:14.010
What we try to do now
is prospective-taking.

01:09:14.010 --> 01:09:16.110
You've gotta be problem-solvers with folks

01:09:16.110 --> 01:09:18.630
who are affected by stuff, not for them.

01:09:18.630 --> 01:09:20.130
That's what this work is doing.

01:09:20.130 --> 01:09:22.140
It's why all the kind of educational stuff

01:09:22.140 --> 01:09:24.150
you're talking about do is so important.

01:09:24.150 --> 01:09:26.220
It's what Tone and I have done together.

01:09:26.220 --> 01:09:29.430
Tone and other people at EGSP made me see,

01:09:29.430 --> 01:09:31.080
I just never thought about it before.

01:09:31.080 --> 01:09:34.140
Those who are making policy
for reentry, all right,

01:09:34.140 --> 01:09:35.640
have no experience with reentry

01:09:35.640 --> 01:09:38.010
at all themselves, all right.

01:09:38.010 --> 01:09:40.110
Being informed by people who are inside

01:09:40.110 --> 01:09:41.490
who are about to come out,

01:09:41.490 --> 01:09:43.830
those who have recently come
out, and their families,

01:09:43.830 --> 01:09:46.290
who actually know something
about what's needed

01:09:46.290 --> 01:09:47.580
is just the obvious way to go.

01:09:47.580 --> 01:09:50.520
In fact, it seems idiotic
to do it any other way,

01:09:50.520 --> 01:09:53.130
and yet we do it every other way but that.

01:09:53.130 --> 01:09:55.680
So I just wanna call on
even more doing things

01:09:55.680 --> 01:09:58.260
with to be able to get better solutions.

01:09:58.260 --> 01:09:59.700
That's the kind of thing I'm inspired

01:09:59.700 --> 01:10:01.470
by today a lot of people doing with,

01:10:01.470 --> 01:10:05.460
and doing with expands our
imagination tremendously.

01:10:05.460 --> 01:10:08.157
So thanks very much for
everyone being here.

01:10:08.157 --> 01:10:10.388
(audience applauding)

01:10:10.388 --> 01:10:12.305
And to you all, thanks.

01:10:14.400 --> 01:10:17.850
- Yeah, let's have
questions for one another

01:10:17.850 --> 01:10:19.440
to one another for a few minutes,

01:10:19.440 --> 01:10:21.390
and then we'll open it to the audience.

01:10:26.490 --> 01:10:30.183
- To answer Ben's question, while inside,

01:10:31.860 --> 01:10:35.103
one of the biggest hurdles to
get over was public safety.

01:10:37.080 --> 01:10:40.380
How do you bring in a chess program

01:10:40.380 --> 01:10:42.930
and play Princeton University students

01:10:42.930 --> 01:10:44.940
when it's a threat to public safety,

01:10:44.940 --> 01:10:46.957
it's threat to the people

01:10:46.957 --> 01:10:48.593
that are coming into the institutions,

01:10:49.440 --> 01:10:53.730
and it's a threat that if
someone takes a hostage

01:10:53.730 --> 01:10:57.570
to the safety of the guards
and the how they run things?

01:10:57.570 --> 01:10:59.670
So what we did was,

01:10:59.670 --> 01:11:03.210
through what we call liberation education,

01:11:03.210 --> 01:11:08.210
we was introduced to a concept
called Public Health, right?

01:11:08.850 --> 01:11:13.850
This is a way of changing the
health of this institution.

01:11:14.010 --> 01:11:14.910
And it's at the time

01:11:14.910 --> 01:11:18.393
I was incarcerated in
Trenton State Prison,

01:11:19.260 --> 01:11:21.030
now known as New Jersey State Prison.

01:11:21.030 --> 01:11:25.020
I did 20 years there, and I
did my last 10 at East Jersey.

01:11:25.020 --> 01:11:27.300
And what wind up happening was,

01:11:27.300 --> 01:11:29.400
when we won that first argument

01:11:29.400 --> 01:11:32.250
by framing it through
a public health lens,

01:11:32.250 --> 01:11:36.150
this'll reduce violence,
this'll reduce different things,

01:11:36.150 --> 01:11:39.870
it'll teach cognitive
thinking, critical engagement.

01:11:39.870 --> 01:11:43.200
It'll do a lot of things just with this.

01:11:43.200 --> 01:11:48.117
So our first, our tournament
was with Princeton.

01:11:49.140 --> 01:11:54.140
We had 60 people, but I think
Trenton is 1,100 people.

01:11:56.280 --> 01:11:57.963
800 signed up.

01:11:59.730 --> 01:12:03.960
And then it became an
instrument to incentivize.

01:12:03.960 --> 01:12:08.960
And we wind up being in, on
ESPN while we were inside.

01:12:12.300 --> 01:12:14.250
Most people don't look
at chess as a sport,

01:12:14.250 --> 01:12:16.563
but chess is a sport, right?

01:12:17.820 --> 01:12:20.550
So when I tell the youth
that I work with right now

01:12:20.550 --> 01:12:23.673
in Rutgers Youth Success
program in Camden,

01:12:25.433 --> 01:12:26.827
I said, "You know I was on the ESPN."

01:12:26.827 --> 01:12:28.310
"Mr. Tone, you cappin'."

01:12:29.430 --> 01:12:30.328
(audience laughing)

01:12:30.328 --> 01:12:33.127
I'm like, "And I was in
'Sports Illustrated'."

01:12:33.127 --> 01:12:37.080
"Yeah, right. Right. You
ain't got no game like that."

01:12:37.080 --> 01:12:38.220
- [Ben] I've got the
article on my computer here

01:12:38.220 --> 01:12:40.414
if you wanna see it later on, yeah.

01:12:40.414 --> 01:12:41.733
- Yeah, so and a lot of people,

01:12:41.733 --> 01:12:43.507
like when I open it up, and it was like,

01:12:43.507 --> 01:12:45.759
"Well, how was y'all able
to do that in there?"

01:12:45.759 --> 01:12:47.787
And I said, "Through the
public health space, right?

01:12:47.787 --> 01:12:50.520
And a harm reduction strategy."

01:12:50.520 --> 01:12:55.260
And it greatly reduced
institutional violence,

01:12:55.260 --> 01:12:58.470
because people wanted to
get into the chess club

01:12:58.470 --> 01:13:02.070
in order to play against
Princeton like the elite,

01:13:02.070 --> 01:13:03.690
like they thought they were something

01:13:03.690 --> 01:13:06.120
and they would do all of that.

01:13:06.120 --> 01:13:10.653
And what wound up happening
was, when I got to East Jersey,

01:13:11.790 --> 01:13:14.280
that's how we introduced the concept

01:13:14.280 --> 01:13:17.040
to the administrators and the commissioner

01:13:17.040 --> 01:13:19.740
of corrections using a
public health argument.

01:13:19.740 --> 01:13:24.740
I've never lost a public
health argument institution.

01:13:25.590 --> 01:13:27.720
Right, and coming home and bringing

01:13:27.720 --> 01:13:32.700
that same carceral
organizing to the community,

01:13:32.700 --> 01:13:34.320
it's like, you know, I remember,

01:13:34.320 --> 01:13:35.700
I've never been to Brooklyn in my life.

01:13:35.700 --> 01:13:38.250
I only seen Brooklyn in
the movies and on TV.

01:13:38.250 --> 01:13:43.250
And I went to Brooklyn, and we
were in this housing project,

01:13:44.430 --> 01:13:49.430
and I remember giving a
presentation and saying,

01:13:49.777 --> 01:13:52.107
"Well, we don't have
to defund the police."

01:13:53.460 --> 01:13:54.293
Right?

01:13:54.293 --> 01:13:56.910
I said, "All we have to do is repurpose

01:13:56.910 --> 01:13:59.190
the money to public health
and address the public health

01:13:59.190 --> 01:14:01.740
and treatment as opposed to punishment."

01:14:01.740 --> 01:14:04.650
And you can continue to do
what you want over here,

01:14:04.650 --> 01:14:07.140
but we are gonna address it
through a public health lens

01:14:07.140 --> 01:14:09.030
and a harm reduction strategy.

01:14:09.030 --> 01:14:10.893
And what wind up happening was,

01:14:11.850 --> 01:14:13.530
one of the side effects of the talks

01:14:13.530 --> 01:14:16.470
is when I got off the
stage and I walked down,

01:14:16.470 --> 01:14:18.000
the police started surrounding me,

01:14:18.000 --> 01:14:19.620
and immediately, I got triggered,

01:14:19.620 --> 01:14:22.410
and I'm thinking, "Oh my
God, what did I say now?"

01:14:22.410 --> 01:14:24.450
Like I'm, you know, here we go.

01:14:24.450 --> 01:14:27.510
And they all came, and
when they surrounded me,

01:14:27.510 --> 01:14:30.720
they was like, "I did not understand

01:14:30.720 --> 01:14:33.150
what you said about, we didn't
have to defund the police.

01:14:33.150 --> 01:14:34.830
Can you explain it?"

01:14:34.830 --> 01:14:38.297
I said, "If we strictly focus on dealing

01:14:41.880 --> 01:14:44.920
with the social issues
through a public health model

01:14:47.400 --> 01:14:49.380
and a harm reduction strategy,

01:14:49.380 --> 01:14:51.060
there's money already sitting there

01:14:51.060 --> 01:14:52.863
in surplus that we're not using.

01:14:54.840 --> 01:14:57.630
There's billions and
billions of dollars there,

01:14:57.630 --> 01:14:59.460
and it's not reaching the communities

01:14:59.460 --> 01:15:00.810
that desperately need them,

01:15:00.810 --> 01:15:03.510
especially black and brown communities."

01:15:03.510 --> 01:15:06.063
So I remember, it was Sergeant Morgan,

01:15:08.010 --> 01:15:09.810
he's probably the only second officer

01:15:09.810 --> 01:15:12.270
that I ever shook hands with.

01:15:12.270 --> 01:15:16.170
And he was like, "Can I
have your phone number

01:15:16.170 --> 01:15:19.440
and can I have your email address?"

01:15:19.440 --> 01:15:22.320
So I'm looking at my guys, Umar Dollard,

01:15:22.320 --> 01:15:25.440
and most of the people that I work

01:15:25.440 --> 01:15:28.860
with have served in
ordinance amount of time.

01:15:28.860 --> 01:15:33.783
The minimum is 28 years.
The highest is 52 years.

01:15:35.010 --> 01:15:38.343
And we imagined, while we were inside,

01:15:39.780 --> 01:15:42.780
we have our own school that we purchase

01:15:42.780 --> 01:15:46.260
from the Board of Education
in the city of Camden.

01:15:46.260 --> 01:15:49.590
We have our own studio,
we have our own cameras,

01:15:49.590 --> 01:15:53.283
green screen production, we
have our own business district.

01:15:54.270 --> 01:15:57.960
But because we imagined
it when it didn't exist

01:15:57.960 --> 01:16:00.570
and came home and manifested

01:16:00.570 --> 01:16:02.640
through a public health agenda

01:16:02.640 --> 01:16:04.383
and a harm reduction strategy.

01:16:08.970 --> 01:16:10.500
- We have a very patient audience.

01:16:10.500 --> 01:16:12.690
I think we should give them a voice now,

01:16:12.690 --> 01:16:13.890
if that's okay with the rest of you.

01:16:13.890 --> 01:16:14.723
- [Ben] Yes, yes, yes, yes.

01:16:14.723 --> 01:16:15.930
- Questions.

01:16:15.930 --> 01:16:16.763
Yes.

01:16:18.120 --> 01:16:19.350
- There's a mic somewhere.

01:16:19.350 --> 01:16:20.573
- Yes, right there. Yeah.

01:16:23.610 --> 01:16:27.150
- [Charlie] It's on?
Hi, my name is Charlie.

01:16:27.150 --> 01:16:28.395
First of all,

01:16:28.395 --> 01:16:29.940
I would like to thank you
guys for having this event.

01:16:29.940 --> 01:16:31.893
I found out about it on Facebook.

01:16:34.380 --> 01:16:37.650
I served about two decades,
came home recently,

01:16:37.650 --> 01:16:39.660
and I'm still shaking off the shackles

01:16:39.660 --> 01:16:41.760
of being institutionalized.

01:16:41.760 --> 01:16:45.810
And I would just like to invite anybody

01:16:45.810 --> 01:16:48.840
that would like to pick my
mind, maybe, through my,

01:16:48.840 --> 01:16:53.610
I have a mountain of experience
and knowledge on this topic,

01:16:53.610 --> 01:16:58.110
and I would just like to
give back in any way I can.

01:16:58.110 --> 01:17:00.840
So if anybody would like to reach

01:17:00.840 --> 01:17:03.240
out and pick my mind, I'm here.

01:17:03.240 --> 01:17:08.240
You can catch me at
charlierockldprisonmentality@gmail.com.

01:17:08.310 --> 01:17:09.143
- Welcome home, Charlie.

01:17:09.143 --> 01:17:10.615
- Thank you, guys.
- Welcome home. Yeah, yeah.

01:17:10.615 --> 01:17:13.782
(audience applauding)

01:17:15.330 --> 01:17:16.163
- Rachel.

01:17:16.163 --> 01:17:18.030
- [Rachel] Yes, a question
for any of the five

01:17:18.030 --> 01:17:19.780
of you who would care to answer it.

01:17:20.730 --> 01:17:24.360
Thanks, those presentations were amazing.

01:17:24.360 --> 01:17:26.790
Just, I sometimes, sorry,

01:17:26.790 --> 01:17:29.040
I was gonna elect myself of
sort of state of Missouri,

01:17:29.040 --> 01:17:33.390
not of the state, but as
a Californian, we have a,

01:17:33.390 --> 01:17:35.790
the scope and particularity
of our prison system

01:17:35.790 --> 01:17:40.260
is maybe somewhat unique, but the,

01:17:40.260 --> 01:17:42.570
you know, it's very unnatural, I think,

01:17:42.570 --> 01:17:45.900
that incarceration involves
a separation of the genders.

01:17:45.900 --> 01:17:49.353
And women's prisons in California,

01:17:50.610 --> 01:17:52.320
I'm sorry to generalize but I'm going to,

01:17:52.320 --> 01:17:53.520
because I would really love to hear

01:17:53.520 --> 01:17:55.710
what you all think about this.

01:17:55.710 --> 01:17:57.570
Like, the culture of learning

01:17:57.570 --> 01:18:02.220
and intellectual strength
and enriching the mind

01:18:02.220 --> 01:18:03.750
and having an interior life

01:18:03.750 --> 01:18:07.140
that is inviable by the
staff and the guards,

01:18:07.140 --> 01:18:12.140
that is not really robust in
California women's prisons.

01:18:12.180 --> 01:18:13.530
And I've had long conversations

01:18:13.530 --> 01:18:15.330
with people inside prison about this,

01:18:15.330 --> 01:18:18.090
and people explain things to
me about why it's not robust,

01:18:18.090 --> 01:18:20.857
and they have real reasons like,

01:18:20.857 --> 01:18:23.790
"You don't understand
what it's like in here.

01:18:23.790 --> 01:18:26.850
Most people come from profound trauma.

01:18:26.850 --> 01:18:28.980
They're trying to order
their mind all the time

01:18:28.980 --> 01:18:33.720
and deal with a kind of
high-drama environment

01:18:33.720 --> 01:18:35.760
where there's eight people
to a room, et cetera,

01:18:35.760 --> 01:18:38.160
et cetera, and no one has privacy."

01:18:38.160 --> 01:18:43.067
And so it is has not been
one of the main avenues

01:18:44.220 --> 01:18:48.930
to a kind of psychic or
mental liberation for people.

01:18:48.930 --> 01:18:50.160
And I was wondering if you could just talk

01:18:50.160 --> 01:18:53.220
about your experiences as
teachers and as students

01:18:53.220 --> 01:18:56.580
and whether this comes up in other places.

01:18:56.580 --> 01:18:58.470
Is California unique in that way?

01:18:58.470 --> 01:19:02.340
And if not, like, how to think

01:19:02.340 --> 01:19:07.340
about this uneven distribution
of forms of liberation

01:19:08.280 --> 01:19:10.623
through intellectual work through reading.

01:19:15.604 --> 01:19:17.490
- Can you address that?
Do you guys do both?

01:19:17.490 --> 01:19:20.520
- So like I said, we
started our programming

01:19:20.520 --> 01:19:23.673
in the federal women's prison today,

01:19:24.600 --> 01:19:25.860
this morning (chuckles).

01:19:25.860 --> 01:19:29.910
People were delivered
their admissions letters.

01:19:29.910 --> 01:19:33.840
So I can't speak with
much expertise about,

01:19:33.840 --> 01:19:36.240
I mean, I am, I'll tell you,

01:19:36.240 --> 01:19:39.363
women's prisons are different, you know.

01:19:41.460 --> 01:19:45.900
We've, you know, already
in just the short while

01:19:45.900 --> 01:19:46.733
that we've been, you know,

01:19:46.733 --> 01:19:51.733
we had information sessions
and ran interviews there,

01:19:51.930 --> 01:19:53.820
but I do think, I mean, I'm not sure

01:19:53.820 --> 01:19:56.130
what security level those prisons are.

01:19:56.130 --> 01:19:58.500
There is, you know, what
you describe sounds similar

01:19:58.500 --> 01:20:00.030
to the environment of this prison

01:20:00.030 --> 01:20:02.610
where we're working, which is essentially,

01:20:02.610 --> 01:20:04.860
I mean, it is literally a warehouse

01:20:04.860 --> 01:20:06.930
that was purchased by
the Bureau of Prisons

01:20:06.930 --> 01:20:09.960
and converted into a satellite location

01:20:09.960 --> 01:20:11.400
for the federal prison.

01:20:11.400 --> 01:20:14.070
And so the women who are there

01:20:14.070 --> 01:20:18.753
are in one large gymnasium type room.

01:20:20.400 --> 01:20:25.400
And so the boundaries
between people are not,

01:20:27.660 --> 01:20:32.660
you know, there's a lot of,
you know, I think, I mean,

01:20:35.937 --> 01:20:38.193
I think, I suspect you might be right,

01:20:39.150 --> 01:20:41.490
but I really don't know in particular,

01:20:41.490 --> 01:20:43.740
but I do think that security level

01:20:43.740 --> 01:20:47.880
has a lot to do with what is
possible and able to happen,

01:20:47.880 --> 01:20:49.620
which is a little bit of an oxymoron,

01:20:49.620 --> 01:20:52.260
'cause we found in the
high maximum adult prison

01:20:52.260 --> 01:20:55.713
where we work, which is, you know, not,

01:20:56.550 --> 01:20:58.890
no prison is where anybody should be,

01:20:58.890 --> 01:21:01.080
or ideal, but it's especially not ideal,

01:21:01.080 --> 01:21:03.900
but people at least have some alone time

01:21:03.900 --> 01:21:07.170
and then they have communal
time in the prison where we are.

01:21:07.170 --> 01:21:11.850
At the women's prison where
women are put in one room,

01:21:11.850 --> 01:21:14.310
there isn't that alone,

01:21:14.310 --> 01:21:15.780
we really are gonna have to build

01:21:15.780 --> 01:21:18.570
it out right, spaces especially.

01:21:18.570 --> 01:21:21.300
Spaces are really
important in this context.

01:21:21.300 --> 01:21:23.670
Finding a space for people to have a group

01:21:23.670 --> 01:21:26.460
that is able to meet not just once a week,

01:21:26.460 --> 01:21:27.840
but every single day.

01:21:27.840 --> 01:21:30.210
You know, like, to be in an environment

01:21:30.210 --> 01:21:32.850
where you can imagine
yourself as a college student,

01:21:32.850 --> 01:21:36.933
not just Wednesday
evenings, but all the time.

01:21:38.130 --> 01:21:42.120
- Yeah, you know, I think it's fair to say

01:21:42.120 --> 01:21:47.120
that women have, within
this space of the invisible,

01:21:48.000 --> 01:21:50.490
women in prison have been the invisible,

01:21:50.490 --> 01:21:53.399
within the invisible throughout much

01:21:53.399 --> 01:21:57.153
of the history since the 1970s.

01:21:58.470 --> 01:22:00.210
And, but I think, you know,

01:22:00.210 --> 01:22:02.530
I think it's gonna be context

01:22:02.530 --> 01:22:06.303
and history and person-specific.

01:22:07.500 --> 01:22:10.200
In New York, a lot of the work

01:22:10.200 --> 01:22:12.690
and the teaching I've been doing at Rikers

01:22:12.690 --> 01:22:15.873
has been at Rose's, which
is the women's facility.

01:22:17.220 --> 01:22:22.220
And there was a big emphasis
on providing courses

01:22:23.310 --> 01:22:26.709
from Columbia at Rose's in particular.

01:22:26.709 --> 01:22:29.820
And the Columbia Justice
and Education program

01:22:29.820 --> 01:22:34.820
in part was built by Kathy
Boudin and Cheryl Wilkins

01:22:35.940 --> 01:22:40.940
and has really dedicated a
lot to the Bedford facility.

01:22:41.430 --> 01:22:46.430
So in part, I think it's gonna
be somewhat path-dependent

01:22:47.370 --> 01:22:49.860
on who it is that's doing the work

01:22:49.860 --> 01:22:52.833
and spearheading the
efforts and stuff, you know.

01:22:55.402 --> 01:22:56.569
- I see a few.

01:23:01.740 --> 01:23:05.670
It's, you raise an interesting question,

01:23:05.670 --> 01:23:10.347
and which is, the irony
is we look to California

01:23:10.347 --> 01:23:13.713
and the writings from
California on the East Coast,

01:23:14.640 --> 01:23:17.580
and they're on the West Coast.

01:23:17.580 --> 01:23:21.750
And what you just proposed was sort

01:23:21.750 --> 01:23:25.170
of what reminds me of
what just happened in the,

01:23:25.170 --> 01:23:26.670
well, it's been happening for quite

01:23:26.670 --> 01:23:29.940
a while in Edna Mahan and New Jersey,

01:23:29.940 --> 01:23:33.090
with the beatings and
the rapes and the things

01:23:33.090 --> 01:23:37.350
of women and where they have
this institution, right?

01:23:37.350 --> 01:23:38.730
So one of the things is,

01:23:38.730 --> 01:23:43.730
what I was anexing in
a lot of our protests

01:23:45.660 --> 01:23:48.540
was the location of the prison, right?

01:23:48.540 --> 01:23:49.590
You treat it the same way

01:23:49.590 --> 01:23:53.640
you treat domestic violence
people, women, and how they do.

01:23:53.640 --> 01:23:55.740
The first thing they did was isolate them.

01:23:56.700 --> 01:23:57.533
Right?

01:23:57.533 --> 01:24:00.510
They put 'em in a far out place
where nobody can get to 'em.

01:24:00.510 --> 01:24:05.400
That's what most DV violators do. Right?

01:24:05.400 --> 01:24:09.960
So the institution is not by
any public transportation.

01:24:09.960 --> 01:24:14.253
You can't get there by
train, bus, or anything,

01:24:15.180 --> 01:24:16.770
and they're remote.

01:24:16.770 --> 01:24:21.480
And that isolation, and
that isolation serves

01:24:21.480 --> 01:24:26.343
as a means of social control
inside the carceral space.

01:24:27.960 --> 01:24:32.280
And when you do it that
way, right? It's by design.

01:24:32.280 --> 01:24:33.630
So what they're manifesting

01:24:33.630 --> 01:24:35.370
and what they're explaining to you.

01:24:35.370 --> 01:24:37.740
So it's like, when I began to,

01:24:37.740 --> 01:24:40.380
we have a program called
Meet Him At The Gate.

01:24:40.380 --> 01:24:42.720
And it started with
anybody that's coming home.

01:24:42.720 --> 01:24:45.090
We go to the institutions
no matter how far.

01:24:45.090 --> 01:24:48.180
Well, New Jersey is a pretty
densely popular state,

01:24:48.180 --> 01:24:51.570
so we can get to all, right
now, I think we have nine,

01:24:51.570 --> 01:24:55.113
we can get to all nine, but
Edna Mahan is the hardest.

01:24:56.430 --> 01:24:59.580
But then I quickly learned
that I was ill-equipped

01:24:59.580 --> 01:25:03.213
to handle the needs of women
coming home from incarceration.

01:25:04.080 --> 01:25:05.310
I don't know what to buy a woman,

01:25:05.310 --> 01:25:06.570
I don't know what a five six

01:25:06.570 --> 01:25:08.640
is or zero or nothing like that.

01:25:08.640 --> 01:25:10.440
I don't know how to do those things.

01:25:10.440 --> 01:25:14.250
So what I did was I began to collaborate

01:25:14.250 --> 01:25:16.290
with Casavere who came home

01:25:16.290 --> 01:25:18.360
who also served time at Edna Mahan,

01:25:18.360 --> 01:25:20.310
and then she took Meet Him at the Gate

01:25:20.310 --> 01:25:22.260
and turned it into Meet Her at the Gate

01:25:23.730 --> 01:25:26.580
and addressed and then was
duplicating what we were doing

01:25:26.580 --> 01:25:29.610
on the male side for the female side.

01:25:29.610 --> 01:25:32.850
And one thing we all have to
understand in this country,

01:25:32.850 --> 01:25:36.030
women are the most underserved population

01:25:36.030 --> 01:25:38.373
in the carceral state of America.

01:25:40.050 --> 01:25:41.553
There's no services for them.

01:25:42.690 --> 01:25:45.090
And that's what you are experiencing.

01:25:45.090 --> 01:25:49.020
And the thing is that that got,
this is what this is about.

01:25:49.020 --> 01:25:51.660
How do we begin to imagine spaces

01:25:51.660 --> 01:25:55.390
or opportunities or tactics and strategies

01:25:56.280 --> 01:26:00.963
to bring forth those
solutions or methodologies?

01:26:10.042 --> 01:26:11.040
- [Audience Member] Yeah,
hi, thank you so much.

01:26:11.040 --> 01:26:11.873
That's loud.

01:26:14.070 --> 01:26:18.360
I've just been thinking about
this kind of all day today.

01:26:18.360 --> 01:26:21.330
And Zelda, you mentioned
reading the mission statement

01:26:21.330 --> 01:26:23.130
through an abolitionist lens.

01:26:23.130 --> 01:26:26.400
Kind of hoping for or
imagining a post-carceral state

01:26:26.400 --> 01:26:29.550
being led by those that are
currently incarcerated today.

01:26:29.550 --> 01:26:34.550
And I'm curious, for most
of you that are working

01:26:34.650 --> 01:26:37.590
at these larger institutions
that are still culpable

01:26:37.590 --> 01:26:41.850
for increased policing in
black and brown neighborhoods

01:26:41.850 --> 01:26:45.003
in under the guise of protecting
mostly white students,

01:26:46.050 --> 01:26:50.490
how do we contend with those two truths

01:26:50.490 --> 01:26:54.510
or multiple truths while
leading these great programs

01:26:54.510 --> 01:26:58.893
and just existing in
those multiple realities?

01:27:06.030 --> 01:27:07.593
- Great question. Thanks.

01:27:14.580 --> 01:27:19.580
You know, it's, that's
obviously a big problem at Yale,

01:27:22.140 --> 01:27:25.413
at Columbia, and many
of these institutions.

01:27:26.760 --> 01:27:29.940
The way I've tried to think

01:27:29.940 --> 01:27:34.530
about it is that at these institutions,

01:27:34.530 --> 01:27:38.980
there are some people who are
doing extraordinary things

01:27:40.230 --> 01:27:41.880
and some great centers,

01:27:41.880 --> 01:27:44.913
centers for justice and others elsewhere.

01:27:46.350 --> 01:27:51.350
And that there are also these

01:27:51.570 --> 01:27:55.170
other spaces that are causing harm.

01:27:55.170 --> 01:28:00.170
And you have to, in a way, I kind of feel

01:28:00.180 --> 01:28:04.473
as if I don't wanna give
up claiming the space

01:28:07.140 --> 01:28:12.140
for the good projects
by seeding the ground

01:28:13.170 --> 01:28:17.163
to the nefast in a way.

01:28:19.050 --> 01:28:22.440
And so, I mean, I try as much as possible

01:28:22.440 --> 01:28:26.930
to promote and foster
and support those spaces

01:28:30.060 --> 01:28:35.060
that are doing good and also
fighting against the other,

01:28:35.430 --> 01:28:39.383
you know, tentacles of these
abominable institutions.

01:28:44.520 --> 01:28:48.333
- I think Judith Resnik had
her hand up in the back there.

01:28:52.290 --> 01:28:54.120
- [Ruth] Thanks, I just wanted to engage

01:28:54.120 --> 01:28:56.490
a little bit more with
the gender question,

01:28:56.490 --> 01:28:57.780
because obviously, the institutions

01:28:57.780 --> 01:29:00.333
are constructed by and construct gender.

01:29:01.350 --> 01:29:05.247
Women are about 10% of the
incarcerated population

01:29:05.247 --> 01:29:07.680
and the stuff we do on
solitary black women

01:29:07.680 --> 01:29:11.040
are way over constrained in
solitary than white women,

01:29:11.040 --> 01:29:14.880
even though solitary is used
less in women's facilities,

01:29:14.880 --> 01:29:17.160
in facilities designated for women

01:29:17.160 --> 01:29:18.960
than facilities designated for men.

01:29:18.960 --> 01:29:22.980
But I'm thinking in terms of Rachel

01:29:22.980 --> 01:29:26.880
and some of the other comments
about in the late '70s,

01:29:26.880 --> 01:29:31.800
the US house held hearings,
women, the forgotten offender,

01:29:31.800 --> 01:29:35.627
apropos the left out,
not much on the screen.

01:29:36.636 --> 01:29:37.527
Though, at the time,

01:29:37.527 --> 01:29:40.590
the federal facilities had
no things called camps,

01:29:40.590 --> 01:29:44.400
which are the least secure
facilities for women at all.

01:29:44.400 --> 01:29:46.410
But after a lot of activism,

01:29:46.410 --> 01:29:49.350
then there became a camp,
actually, at Danbury

01:29:49.350 --> 01:29:52.410
and then a women's facility
for about 1,100 people

01:29:52.410 --> 01:29:53.790
till the Federal Bureau of Prisons

01:29:53.790 --> 01:29:57.480
in about 2012, '13 decided to move,

01:29:57.480 --> 01:30:00.810
proposed to move 1,000 women
to Aliceville, Alabama,

01:30:00.810 --> 01:30:03.060
which is a bad place to
be if you're from Alabama,

01:30:03.060 --> 01:30:04.080
let alone elsewhere,

01:30:04.080 --> 01:30:08.070
because it's extremely remote
near the Mississippi border.

01:30:08.070 --> 01:30:11.970
So the women actually who
remain, a lot of fight,

01:30:11.970 --> 01:30:14.580
and a small facility for women remains,

01:30:14.580 --> 01:30:16.680
and in the wake of COVID,

01:30:16.680 --> 01:30:21.420
the, and about 100 people
got out on home confinement.

01:30:21.420 --> 01:30:24.750
There's a group called
the Danbury 100 organized

01:30:24.750 --> 01:30:27.750
by these amazing women
who have pulled together

01:30:27.750 --> 01:30:30.960
and connected people who
are on home confinement

01:30:30.960 --> 01:30:33.870
and are a big fight about
being reincarcerated there.

01:30:33.870 --> 01:30:36.960
So I was wondering about
whether the activism engagement

01:30:36.960 --> 01:30:40.830
for women is differently
engaged, expressed,

01:30:40.830 --> 01:30:45.630
and/or seen even when it
exists and/or suppressed.

01:30:45.630 --> 01:30:49.170
And also what the sort
of library resources are,

01:30:49.170 --> 01:30:51.390
including the Inside-Out stuff

01:30:51.390 --> 01:30:56.390
as well as the ways Kathy Boudin

01:30:56.520 --> 01:30:59.310
and other people's activism at Bedford

01:30:59.310 --> 01:31:01.770
was instrumental in
transforming the facility

01:31:01.770 --> 01:31:04.170
after an enormous amount of fighting,

01:31:04.170 --> 01:31:06.180
but she also had a huge amount of access

01:31:06.180 --> 01:31:10.680
to resources to make visible
her conflicts around it.

01:31:10.680 --> 01:31:15.510
So one of the projects for
the narrative imagination

01:31:15.510 --> 01:31:18.598
would be to try to probe Rachel's

01:31:18.598 --> 01:31:22.083
how and where and when is that,

01:31:22.980 --> 01:31:26.190
are women's facilities
underseen, underserved,

01:31:26.190 --> 01:31:29.640
underengaged, reengaged,
or differently engaged?

01:31:29.640 --> 01:31:34.640
And how might one make more
accessible the work from the,

01:31:35.280 --> 01:31:37.170
the work that's going on inside, outside,

01:31:37.170 --> 01:31:39.603
as well as outside work going inside?

01:31:43.050 --> 01:31:48.050
- One of the biggest hurdles
is when women come home

01:31:48.660 --> 01:31:53.340
and they enter into relationships, right?

01:31:53.340 --> 01:31:57.183
And most of their partners
force them back into silence.

01:31:58.560 --> 01:31:59.887
Right?

01:31:59.887 --> 01:32:01.350
They don't want anybody to know

01:32:01.350 --> 01:32:05.070
that their significant
other, loved one, partner,

01:32:05.070 --> 01:32:08.493
wife, fiance has been incarcerated.

01:32:10.680 --> 01:32:13.200
So you go from an environment that does

01:32:13.200 --> 01:32:16.170
that to coming home and
being in a relationship

01:32:16.170 --> 01:32:19.140
that also continues that same process.

01:32:19.140 --> 01:32:22.140
So when we're out in front of Edna Mahan

01:32:22.140 --> 01:32:24.240
and we're advocating,
we're calling for services,

01:32:24.240 --> 01:32:27.210
we're calling for not
reform but transformation

01:32:27.210 --> 01:32:31.830
of a system that isolates
and abuses with impunity.

01:32:31.830 --> 01:32:34.623
It was hard to organize amongst the women.

01:32:36.660 --> 01:32:39.390
And when the ones that did come out,

01:32:39.390 --> 01:32:40.770
that was the first time,

01:32:40.770 --> 01:32:44.820
it was like a history in New
Jersey to be able to do that.

01:32:44.820 --> 01:32:48.540
And I understand like,
that stigma is harder on,

01:32:48.540 --> 01:32:50.280
I had to learn that it was harder

01:32:50.280 --> 01:32:52.113
on women than it was on men.

01:32:53.040 --> 01:32:57.600
Why, because they're the
mothers, they're the daughters.

01:32:57.600 --> 01:33:01.740
Right, that gender-specific
what you ask is a lot of things

01:33:01.740 --> 01:33:05.493
that become what we call a
new entry pop up barriers.

01:33:06.420 --> 01:33:08.280
Right, to being able to tell that story,

01:33:08.280 --> 01:33:13.280
to be able to go before a
Senate hearing or Capitol Hill,

01:33:13.290 --> 01:33:15.510
wherever you have to go, right,

01:33:15.510 --> 01:33:19.320
it's almost like they're
sworn into secrecy and silence

01:33:19.320 --> 01:33:22.260
even after the custodial
term has been served.

01:33:22.260 --> 01:33:24.420
And that's something
that we have to imagine.

01:33:24.420 --> 01:33:26.160
What can we do to bring them out

01:33:26.160 --> 01:33:28.110
and to bring them in safe spaces

01:33:28.110 --> 01:33:29.700
where they can tell their stories,

01:33:29.700 --> 01:33:32.880
if anonymously or specifically,

01:33:32.880 --> 01:33:36.030
without any other harm occurring to 'em.

01:33:36.030 --> 01:33:37.830
And that's the dilemma that we face.

01:33:38.760 --> 01:33:40.230
- Very interesting.

01:33:40.230 --> 01:33:42.330
Take one more question in the back there.

01:33:42.330 --> 01:33:43.983
You've been very patient waiting.

01:33:45.390 --> 01:33:46.613
- [Audience Member] Thank you.

01:33:49.937 --> 01:33:51.317
I just wanted to expand on the question

01:33:51.317 --> 01:33:53.220
of the previous person.

01:33:53.220 --> 01:33:54.720
My name's Idamu,

01:33:54.720 --> 01:33:59.720
and I served over 15 years
in California state prisons.

01:34:01.530 --> 01:34:06.530
And my question is around
how y'all as educators

01:34:09.690 --> 01:34:11.280
that go inside of prisons or run programs

01:34:11.280 --> 01:34:14.100
that go inside of prison balance,

01:34:14.100 --> 01:34:16.710
I heard one person talk about using

01:34:16.710 --> 01:34:20.400
an abolitionist lens or
abolitionist mission,

01:34:20.400 --> 01:34:25.087
how do you balance the
tension between that

01:34:26.010 --> 01:34:29.880
and the fact that
educational programs are,

01:34:29.880 --> 01:34:32.250
can be considered a reform?

01:34:32.250 --> 01:34:36.420
And so, yeah, just that reforms

01:34:36.420 --> 01:34:38.340
in fact strengthen the system,

01:34:38.340 --> 01:34:40.370
that, you know, the history of prisons

01:34:40.370 --> 01:34:41.973
is a history of reforms.

01:34:43.410 --> 01:34:44.880
Just a quick story.

01:34:44.880 --> 01:34:48.570
I went and visited Norway this summer

01:34:48.570 --> 01:34:50.160
and toured their prison system.

01:34:50.160 --> 01:34:54.030
And what I saw there was
a lot of the same things.

01:34:54.030 --> 01:34:59.030
I mean, Norway has, in
some ways, done prisons

01:34:59.610 --> 01:35:01.020
in a way that's more palatable

01:35:01.020 --> 01:35:03.270
for their society and for the world.

01:35:03.270 --> 01:35:04.980
And what I saw was like a lot

01:35:04.980 --> 01:35:06.930
of different countries sending contingents

01:35:06.930 --> 01:35:08.943
there to tour their system,

01:35:10.200 --> 01:35:13.020
to bring it back to their countries

01:35:13.020 --> 01:35:15.363
so that they could like quell rebellion.

01:35:17.160 --> 01:35:22.110
And so I just think about,
you know, for myself,

01:35:22.110 --> 01:35:25.350
like education inside was a place

01:35:25.350 --> 01:35:27.120
just like one of y'all talked about,

01:35:27.120 --> 01:35:28.800
where it felt non-carceral,

01:35:28.800 --> 01:35:30.840
it felt like we could share ideas

01:35:30.840 --> 01:35:34.020
and there wasn't the threat
of, you know, prison guards

01:35:34.020 --> 01:35:38.013
and there was some level
of equity between students,

01:35:39.870 --> 01:35:43.350
but that also the prison
uses that as a way

01:35:43.350 --> 01:35:46.080
to strengthen itself and to
show like what we're doing

01:35:46.080 --> 01:35:48.060
and what, you know, like
these are the advancements

01:35:48.060 --> 01:35:49.350
that we're making.

01:35:49.350 --> 01:35:52.200
And like if we have an
abolitionist vision,

01:35:52.200 --> 01:35:53.520
like, our ultimate goal is not

01:35:53.520 --> 01:35:56.580
to have better universities
inside of prisons,

01:35:56.580 --> 01:35:58.730
but to like not have
prisons at all, right?

01:36:00.270 --> 01:36:01.320
- Yeah.

01:36:01.320 --> 01:36:02.460
- [Audience Member] And then like just to,

01:36:02.460 --> 01:36:05.074
sorry, just, maybe that's good enough.

01:36:05.074 --> 01:36:06.260
- No, no, no. I wanna hear.

01:36:06.260 --> 01:36:07.620
- But I was just gonna
say that also like Yale

01:36:08.460 --> 01:36:11.220
is not just an institution
that has a police force,

01:36:11.220 --> 01:36:14.070
but also Yale is an institution

01:36:14.070 --> 01:36:16.290
that functions in a lot
of ways like the prison.

01:36:16.290 --> 01:36:19.740
It excludes certain people
and includes certain people

01:36:19.740 --> 01:36:21.660
just in the same way that prison's doing.

01:36:21.660 --> 01:36:26.283
So like that's also a tension
that exists too, right?

01:36:28.015 --> 01:36:29.120
- Yes.
- So.

01:36:29.120 --> 01:36:31.440
- I mean, the short answer is yes.

01:36:31.440 --> 01:36:33.723
All of that is absolutely right.

01:36:34.764 --> 01:36:37.290
But to answer the first
question, you know,

01:36:37.290 --> 01:36:41.826
I think the way that I would answer right

01:36:41.826 --> 01:36:46.050
is it a reformist reform or
is it a non-reformist reform?

01:36:46.050 --> 01:36:47.850
I simply believe that what we're doing

01:36:47.850 --> 01:36:49.170
is a non-reformist reform,

01:36:49.170 --> 01:36:51.690
and the shortest way I
have of explaining that

01:36:51.690 --> 01:36:56.040
is that it helps people
get outta prison, you know.

01:36:56.040 --> 01:36:59.220
Like you heard this story,
you heard Antonne's story.

01:36:59.220 --> 01:37:03.420
We have students who were
sentenced to life in prison,

01:37:03.420 --> 01:37:08.010
you know, and who were able to go through,

01:37:08.010 --> 01:37:09.510
you know, up to a parole board

01:37:09.510 --> 01:37:12.390
or get a sentence
modification or a commutation

01:37:12.390 --> 01:37:15.900
and for whom, you know,
having a Yale transcript

01:37:15.900 --> 01:37:18.000
or showing that they
could enroll right away

01:37:18.000 --> 01:37:20.940
at UNH was not a small thing, you know,

01:37:20.940 --> 01:37:25.200
and truly led to people
being freed from prison.

01:37:25.200 --> 01:37:28.960
And to me, investing them
with that sort of freedom,

01:37:30.570 --> 01:37:34.080
but also, you know, power
to then be in the world

01:37:34.080 --> 01:37:39.080
and making, you know, the
advancements towards abolition,

01:37:39.600 --> 01:37:41.940
you know, towards, you know,

01:37:41.940 --> 01:37:45.210
and I really appreciated
actually what Bernard said

01:37:45.210 --> 01:37:49.500
about, you know, this,
it's a battle, right?

01:37:49.500 --> 01:37:51.540
Yale isn't one thing, you know.

01:37:51.540 --> 01:37:55.980
We all carry this label with us,

01:37:55.980 --> 01:37:58.290
and then you have this responsibility

01:37:58.290 --> 01:38:00.890
of acting with it
attached to you, you know.

01:38:00.890 --> 01:38:02.730
it is a stigma, right?

01:38:02.730 --> 01:38:04.290
Just that you compared Yale to the prison,

01:38:04.290 --> 01:38:06.510
it is also a stigma, just like the prison.

01:38:06.510 --> 01:38:08.910
And when we first started, you know,

01:38:08.910 --> 01:38:11.880
researching for this
program, I would go to every,

01:38:11.880 --> 01:38:15.150
you know, there's a women's
resettlement working group

01:38:15.150 --> 01:38:16.860
and a reentry round table in New Haven.

01:38:16.860 --> 01:38:18.360
And I'd go to every, you know,

01:38:18.360 --> 01:38:21.450
one of these meetings
with my friend, James,

01:38:21.450 --> 01:38:25.350
who was an alum of the Wesleyan
program and they would yell.

01:38:25.350 --> 01:38:27.547
People from the community
would yell at us.

01:38:27.547 --> 01:38:29.370
"Yale, you did this to me.

01:38:29.370 --> 01:38:31.860
You did this to me. You did this to me."

01:38:31.860 --> 01:38:36.127
And in a way like, yes, we
had to sit there and say like,

01:38:36.127 --> 01:38:39.570
"Yes, I have benefited
from this institution

01:38:39.570 --> 01:38:40.800
that has done a lot of,

01:38:40.800 --> 01:38:43.170
has done every single
thing you said, you know."

01:38:43.170 --> 01:38:46.470
And yet we're trying
to build out something

01:38:46.470 --> 01:38:49.440
that can be a part of this, you know,

01:38:49.440 --> 01:38:51.690
if you're imagining a
battle of good and evil,

01:38:51.690 --> 01:38:56.220
but at least, you know, you're
staking more and more people

01:38:56.220 --> 01:39:00.690
for this side of a cause that
is moving towards, you know,

01:39:00.690 --> 01:39:04.080
ideally not building a perfect
university in a prison,

01:39:04.080 --> 01:39:06.120
but building, you know,

01:39:06.120 --> 01:39:08.040
building away from having the prison,

01:39:08.040 --> 01:39:10.470
moving people outside of there,

01:39:10.470 --> 01:39:13.470
investing, you know power
and leadership potential

01:39:13.470 --> 01:39:16.410
into those, you know, students who were,

01:39:16.410 --> 01:39:18.060
you know, identifying

01:39:18.060 --> 01:39:20.730
and investing all of our energy and money

01:39:20.730 --> 01:39:22.083
and everything else into.

01:39:23.040 --> 01:39:25.170
- Really quickly, I mean,

01:39:25.170 --> 01:39:27.810
I think all of these things
are double-edged swords

01:39:27.810 --> 01:39:29.730
and they're complicated.

01:39:29.730 --> 01:39:33.960
Conversion stories are
double-edged swords as well, right?

01:39:33.960 --> 01:39:37.200
Because not everyone,
because for all the people

01:39:37.200 --> 01:39:39.090
who've gone through conversion stories,

01:39:39.090 --> 01:39:40.350
as Caleb was telling us,

01:39:40.350 --> 01:39:42.870
I mean, or if the limits
of conversion stories

01:39:42.870 --> 01:39:46.200
is all of those who've been
so damaged by the prison

01:39:46.200 --> 01:39:48.960
that there isn't a conversion story.

01:39:48.960 --> 01:39:52.800
And education also, all those
who, and it becomes something,

01:39:52.800 --> 01:39:54.900
it becomes the test or the way,

01:39:54.900 --> 01:39:57.240
or you know a way to get out of prison,

01:39:57.240 --> 01:40:00.930
but it also fails for so many others.

01:40:00.930 --> 01:40:04.500
So these are really double-edged swords

01:40:04.500 --> 01:40:06.567
and complicated questions.

01:40:06.567 --> 01:40:07.980
And I'm someone actually

01:40:07.980 --> 01:40:10.980
who doesn't believe in
non-reformist reform.

01:40:10.980 --> 01:40:14.130
I think non-reformist
reform is just an oxymoron,

01:40:14.130 --> 01:40:16.950
it's just an easy way out
of a complicated situation

01:40:16.950 --> 01:40:18.870
as an abolitionist.

01:40:18.870 --> 01:40:22.140
But I would say that
as a personal, ethical,

01:40:22.140 --> 01:40:26.760
as my own ethical responsibility,

01:40:26.760 --> 01:40:30.167
I cannot simply give up on friends

01:40:32.070 --> 01:40:35.973
and people who are in prison.

01:40:37.260 --> 01:40:40.020
And even if it's gonna be, and even,

01:40:40.020 --> 01:40:42.030
and so there's a point
at which there's just

01:40:42.030 --> 01:40:46.997
an ethical responsibility
to care for others.

01:40:48.000 --> 01:40:51.700
And one way we can do
it is through reading

01:40:52.650 --> 01:40:55.620
and sharing and talking and thinking.

01:40:55.620 --> 01:40:58.650
And so I don't view it as
a non-reformist reform.

01:40:58.650 --> 01:41:02.010
I don't view revolution
as where you gotta come

01:41:02.010 --> 01:41:03.570
to the situation where it's really

01:41:03.570 --> 01:41:06.360
so bad that people are gonna revolt.

01:41:06.360 --> 01:41:11.360
I think you do abolitionist
work and you also care.

01:41:12.300 --> 01:41:16.263
And that means going in and sharing.

01:41:17.610 --> 01:41:21.927
- Abolition, I think that's
a good note on which to end.

01:41:21.927 --> 01:41:25.710
And I refer us back to Antonne's notion

01:41:25.710 --> 01:41:30.710
of liberation education
as an imaginative ideal.

01:41:31.020 --> 01:41:34.110
But I also think of the
fact that of the class

01:41:34.110 --> 01:41:38.190
of 25 that Antonne was a member of,

01:41:38.190 --> 01:41:43.020
so far as I know, 23 of them
still are in East Jersey.

01:41:43.020 --> 01:41:45.990
And so liberation is a great ideal

01:41:45.990 --> 01:41:47.883
and it also has its limitations.

01:41:48.930 --> 01:41:53.610
Listen, we reconvene in
15 minutes at four o'clock

01:41:53.610 --> 01:41:57.390
for our readings by Randall
Horton and Richard Rivera.

01:41:57.390 --> 01:41:59.430
And after that, when they're done,

01:41:59.430 --> 01:42:02.620
there'll be a reception
upstairs in room 131

01:42:03.649 --> 01:42:05.913
to which you are all warmly invited.

01:42:07.162 --> 01:42:09.341
(audience applauding)

01:42:09.341 --> 01:42:10.793
